window.SPEAKUP_B2_BLUEPRINT=[{"id":"su-b2-l1","number":1,"title":"Getting Acquainted","outcome":"Introduce yourself appropriately in two different contexts and sustain a natural conversation with follow-up questions and reactions.","grammarFocus":"Present Simple","grammarRule":"Use Present Simple accurately for whatsapp class introduction. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"WhatsApp class introduction","vocabulary":["background","routine","interest","experience","confident","achievement"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “background” to its meaning.","options":["past education and experience","something you want to know more about","sure of your ability"],"answer":"past education and experience","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “routine” to its meaning.","options":["regular way of doing something","knowledge gained by doing or seeing something","something important completed successfully"],"answer":"regular way of doing something","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “interest” to its meaning.","options":["something you want to know more about","sure of your ability","past education and experience"],"answer":"something you want to know more about","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “experience” to its meaning.","options":["knowledge gained by doing or seeing something","something important completed successfully","regular way of doing something"],"answer":"knowledge gained by doing or seeing something","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “confident” to its meaning.","options":["sure of your ability","past education and experience","something you want to know more about"],"answer":"sure of your ability","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “achievement” to its meaning.","options":["something important completed successfully","regular way of doing something","knowledge gained by doing or seeing something"],"answer":"something important completed successfully","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “regular way of doing something”?","options":["routine","experience","achievement"],"answer":"routine","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: sure of your ability?","options":["confident","background","interest"],"answer":"confident","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “past education and experience”.","answer":"background","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “Her international ___ includes study and work in three countries.”","options":["background","routine","interest"],"answer":"background","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"Hi! My name is Sarah. I'm twenty-eight years old. I live in London, but my hometown is Manchester. I work as a nurse. In my free time I enjoy hiking and reading mystery books. I also love travelling because I enjoy meeting new people. At a professional event, however, Sarah changes the way she introduces herself. Instead of listing facts, she explains that she moved to London for a nursing role that gives her more responsibility and regular contact with international patients. When someone mentions hiking, she does not immediately return to talking about herself. She asks where they usually go, reacts to the answer, and then connects it to one of her own experiences. Sarah has learned that a strong introduction is not a memorised speech. It is a short opening that gives the other person something useful to respond to. She also pays attention to context: with a new classmate she sounds relaxed, while at work she chooses more professional language.","audioScript":"Amina: Hi, I don't think we've met. I'm Amina. I work for a travel company and I'm studying English in the evenings. Yusuf: Nice to meet you. I'm Yusuf. What made you decide to study now? Amina: Most of my customers are international, so I want to explain things more confidently. How about you? Yusuf: I'm preparing to study business at university. I've also become interested in technology. Hodan: Sorry to interrupt, but I heard you mention technology. I've just moved here and I'm hoping to meet people with similar interests. Amina: Then we already have something in common. What kind of technology are you interested in?","questions":[{"q":"Why does Sarah change the way she introduces herself at a professional event?","options":["To match the context and explain her role more meaningfully","To avoid mentioning her work","To use the same memorised speech everywhere"],"answer":"To match the context and explain her role more meaningfully","tag":"reading:purpose"},{"q":"What does Sarah do when another person mentions hiking?","options":["She asks about their experience before connecting it to her own","She immediately changes the subject","She gives a long speech about herself"],"answer":"She asks about their experience before connecting it to her own","tag":"reading:interaction"},{"q":"What is the text's main idea about a strong introduction?","options":["It should open a conversation rather than function as a memorised speech","It should include every personal fact","It should always sound formal"],"answer":"It should open a conversation rather than function as a memorised speech","tag":"reading:main-idea"},{"q":"What does Sarah mainly change when moving from a classmate to a work context?","options":["Her level of formality and professional detail","Her name and nationality","Her interest in other people"],"answer":"Her level of formality and professional detail","tag":"reading:inference"},{"q":"Where does Amina work?","options":["At a travel company","At a university","At a hospital"],"answer":"At a travel company","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"Why is Amina studying English?","options":["To communicate more confidently with international customers","To change her job immediately","To teach technology"],"answer":"To communicate more confidently with international customers","tag":"listening:reason"},{"q":"What is Yusuf preparing to do?","options":["Study business at university","Move to another country for nursing","Start a travel company"],"answer":"Study business at university","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What helps the three learners move beyond basic introductions?","options":["They ask and respond to follow-up questions","They memorise the same speech","They avoid talking about shared interests"],"answer":"They ask and respond to follow-up questions","tag":"listening:interaction"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Hi everyone,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Hi everyone,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to whatsapp class introduction","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to whatsapp class introduction","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “something important completed successfully”?","options":["achievement","routine","experience"],"answer":"achievement","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Thanks — I look forward to hearing from you.","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Thanks — I look forward to hearing from you.","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"You have just joined a new English class WhatsApp group. Write 50–70 words introducing yourself naturally.","minWords":70,"maxWords":90,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle Present Simple and familiar personal-information language so learners can enter the lesson confidently.","b2Lift":"Extend answers, build rapport, adapt introductions to context, reformulate ideas, and ask purposeful follow-up questions.","performance":"Introduce yourself appropriately in two different contexts and sustain a natural conversation with follow-up questions and reactions.","pronunciation":"Use thought groups and sentence stress so longer introductions remain easy to follow.","mediation":"Listen to a partner's introduction and introduce that person accurately to someone else.","functions":["introduce yourself appropriately","extend an answer with relevant detail","ask purposeful follow-up questions"],"discourse":["opening and extending a turn","linking related personal information","reformulating when meaning is unclear"],"chunks":["professional background","current priorities","shared interest","build rapport","have something in common"],"interactionExpressions":["What brings you here?","How did you get into that?","If I understood you correctly, ..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"You are introducing yourself at a conference. Which sentence sounds most natural for a stable fact?","options":["I work in education and specialise in teacher training.","I am working in education since 2019.","I have worked yesterday in education."],"answer":"I work in education and specialise in teacher training.","tag":"grammar:meaning"},{"q":"Which question correctly asks about someone's usual work?","options":["What do you do?","What are you doing usually?","What have you done every day?"],"answer":"What do you do?","tag":"grammar:function"},{"q":"Choose the best sentence for a regular habit, not a temporary project.","options":["I usually meet international clients on Mondays.","I am usually meeting international clients on Mondays.","I have met international clients on Mondays this month."],"answer":"I usually meet international clients on Mondays.","tag":"grammar:aspect"},{"q":"Which sentence correctly describes a general preference?","options":["I prefer smaller groups because conversation feels more natural.","I am preferring smaller groups because conversation feels more natural.","I have preferred smaller groups right now."],"answer":"I prefer smaller groups because conversation feels more natural.","tag":"grammar:state-verb"},{"q":"A learner says, “I live in Borama, but this month I ___ with relatives in Hargeisa.” Choose the best form.","options":["am staying","stay","have stayed"],"answer":"am staying","tag":"grammar:contrast"},{"q":"Which pair shows the clearest contrast between a general fact and a current temporary situation?","options":["I teach English, but this week I'm attending a training course.","I am teaching English generally, but this week I attend a training course.","I have taught English generally, but this week I teach a training course."],"answer":"I teach English, but this week I'm attending a training course.","tag":"grammar:contrast"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l2","number":2,"title":"Work & Careers","outcome":"Take part in a career panel, present a credible career story, and support claims with specific evidence and lessons learned.","grammarFocus":"Present Perfect vs Past Simple","grammarRule":"Use Present Perfect vs Past Simple accurately for professional email about experience. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Professional email about experience","vocabulary":["promotion","workload","qualification","deadline","responsibility","achievement"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “promotion” to its meaning.","options":["move to a higher job","an official skill or achievement that makes someone suitable","duty you manage"],"answer":"move to a higher job","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “workload” to its meaning.","options":["the amount of work a person must do","latest time work must finish","something important completed successfully"],"answer":"the amount of work a person must do","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “qualification” to its meaning.","options":["an official skill or achievement that makes someone suitable","duty you manage","move to a higher job"],"answer":"an official skill or achievement that makes someone suitable","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “deadline” to its meaning.","options":["latest time work must finish","something important completed successfully","the amount of work a person must do"],"answer":"latest time work must finish","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “responsibility” to its meaning.","options":["duty you manage","move to a higher job","an official skill or achievement that makes someone suitable"],"answer":"duty you manage","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “achievement” to its meaning.","options":["something important completed successfully","the amount of work a person must do","latest time work must finish"],"answer":"something important completed successfully","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “the amount of work a person must do”?","options":["workload","deadline","achievement"],"answer":"workload","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: duty you manage?","options":["responsibility","promotion","qualification"],"answer":"responsibility","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “move to a higher job”.","answer":"promotion","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “The team must meet the ___ by Friday.”","options":["deadline","promotion","workload"],"answer":"deadline","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"Amina joined the company as an intern five years ago. Since then, she has completed two major projects and has been promoted twice. Last year, she managed a team of six people for the first time. She has also started mentoring new employees, something she never expected to enjoy as much as she does now. Her progress was not automatic. During her second year, Amina missed an important internal deadline because she had underestimated the amount of coordination the project required. Instead of hiding the mistake, she explained what had happened, created a new tracking system, and asked a senior colleague to review the plan. The project was eventually delivered successfully, and the new system was later adopted by another team. Amina now says that the experience mattered more than the promotion that followed it because it changed the way she manages responsibility. When she talks about her career, she tries not to make vague claims such as 'I am a good leader'. She gives an example, describes what she did, and explains the result.","audioScript":"Interviewer: You have moved quite quickly from intern to team leader. What do you think made the biggest difference? Amina: Probably learning to show evidence rather than simply saying I was ready for more responsibility. Last year I coordinated a project involving six colleagues and two external suppliers. Interviewer: Was everything successful? Amina: Not at first. We missed an early deadline, so I changed the schedule and introduced weekly progress checks. The final report was submitted on time, and the client renewed the contract. Interviewer: What did you take from that? Amina: That leadership is less about having all the answers and more about making problems visible early enough to solve them.","questions":[{"q":"What mistake did Amina make during her second year?","options":["She underestimated the coordination needed and missed an internal deadline","She resigned without warning","She refused to work with colleagues"],"answer":"She underestimated the coordination needed and missed an internal deadline","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"How did she respond to the mistake?","options":["She explained it, created a tracking system, and asked for review","She hid it from the team","She abandoned the project"],"answer":"She explained it, created a tracking system, and asked for review","tag":"reading:response"},{"q":"Why does Amina say the experience mattered more than the promotion?","options":["It changed how she manages responsibility","It increased her salary immediately","It allowed her to avoid deadlines"],"answer":"It changed how she manages responsibility","tag":"reading:inference"},{"q":"What communication principle does Amina now follow when describing achievements?","options":["Give a specific example and explain the result","Use broad claims without evidence","Focus only on job titles"],"answer":"Give a specific example and explain the result","tag":"reading:main-idea"},{"q":"How many colleagues were involved in Amina's project?","options":["Six","Two","Twelve"],"answer":"Six","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What problem occurred early in the project?","options":["The team missed an early deadline","The client cancelled immediately","The suppliers refused to participate"],"answer":"The team missed an early deadline","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What change did Amina make?","options":["She introduced weekly progress checks","She reduced all communication","She removed the project schedule"],"answer":"She introduced weekly progress checks","tag":"listening:response"},{"q":"What does Amina say leadership is mainly about?","options":["Making problems visible early enough to solve them","Avoiding every mistake","Having all the answers"],"answer":"Making problems visible early enough to solve them","tag":"listening:stance"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Dear Sir or Madam,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to professional email about experience","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to professional email about experience","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “something important completed successfully”?","options":["achievement","workload","deadline"],"answer":"achievement","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Kind regards,","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Kind regards,","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write a 90–120 word professional email introducing your experience and asking about an opportunity.","minWords":90,"maxWords":120,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle Present Perfect and Past Simple to organise familiar career history.","b2Lift":"Describe achievements with evidence, explain impact, handle career transitions, and use professional collocations precisely.","performance":"Take part in a career panel, present a credible career story, and support claims with specific evidence and lessons learned.","pronunciation":"Stress the result or impact in achievement statements and pause clearly between stages of a career story.","mediation":"Listen to a partner's career story and summarise the key achievement and lesson for the group.","functions":["describe career development","support achievements with evidence","explain lessons learned from experience"],"discourse":["sequencing a career narrative","moving from claim to evidence","highlighting impact"],"chunks":["take on responsibility","meet a deadline","gain hands-on experience","career progression","move into a role"],"interactionExpressions":["What led you to that role?","What was the result?","What did you learn from that experience?"],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Amina still works at the company. Choose the best sentence.","options":["She has worked there for five years.","She worked there for five years.","She had worked there for five years."],"answer":"She has worked there for five years.","tag":"grammar:unfinished-time"},{"q":"The project ended last year. Which sentence is best?","options":["She led a team of six people last year.","She has led a team of six people last year.","She has been leading a team of six people last year."],"answer":"She led a team of six people last year.","tag":"grammar:finished-time"},{"q":"Which sentence focuses on career experience without saying exactly when it happened?","options":["I've managed international projects before.","I managed international projects in 2024.","I had managed international projects last Tuesday."],"answer":"I've managed international projects before.","tag":"grammar:experience"},{"q":"Choose the sentence that explains a present result from recent experience.","options":["I've learned to raise problems earlier, so I handle deadlines better now.","I learned to raise problems earlier in 2022, so the time is unspecified.","I had learned to raise problems now."],"answer":"I've learned to raise problems earlier, so I handle deadlines better now.","tag":"grammar:present-result"},{"q":"Which question is best if the interviewer wants a specific past example?","options":["When did you first lead a team?","Have you first led a team when?","How long did you have led a team?"],"answer":"When did you first lead a team?","tag":"grammar:function"},{"q":"Complete the contrast: “I ___ several teams in my career, but I ___ my largest team last year.”","options":["have managed / managed","managed / have managed","have been managing / have managed"],"answer":"have managed / managed","tag":"grammar:contrast"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l3","number":3,"title":"Travel & Adventure","outcome":"Tell an extended travel story with clear sequencing, evaluation, and spontaneous responses to listener questions.","grammarFocus":"Narrative Tenses","grammarRule":"Use Narrative Tenses accurately for whatsapp travel update. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"WhatsApp travel update","vocabulary":["destination","journey","delay","route","adventure","local"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “destination” to its meaning.","options":["place you are travelling to","something happening later than planned","an unusual or exciting experience"],"answer":"place you are travelling to","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “journey” to its meaning.","options":["travel from one place to another","the way used to travel from one place to another","connected with a nearby place or community"],"answer":"travel from one place to another","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “delay” to its meaning.","options":["something happening later than planned","an unusual or exciting experience","place you are travelling to"],"answer":"something happening later than planned","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “route” to its meaning.","options":["the way used to travel from one place to another","connected with a nearby place or community","travel from one place to another"],"answer":"the way used to travel from one place to another","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “adventure” to its meaning.","options":["an unusual or exciting experience","place you are travelling to","something happening later than planned"],"answer":"an unusual or exciting experience","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “local” to its meaning.","options":["connected with a nearby place or community","travel from one place to another","the way used to travel from one place to another"],"answer":"connected with a nearby place or community","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “travel from one place to another”?","options":["journey","route","local"],"answer":"journey","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: an unusual or exciting experience?","options":["adventure","destination","delay"],"answer":"adventure","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “place you are travelling to”.","answer":"destination","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “We have a six-hour ___ before our next flight.”","options":["layover","destination","journey"],"answer":"layover","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"We had been hiking for three hours when the fog suddenly rolled in. We couldn't see the path anymore, and I realised we had wandered off the main trail. My friend was checking her phone, but there was no signal. Just as we were starting to panic, we heard voices — a group of local shepherds had been watching us from a distance and came to guide us back safely. The experience also changed the way they prepare for trips. Before that day, they had relied almost completely on their phones for directions and had not told anyone exactly which route they planned to take. Later, they realised that the problem was not simply the fog; several small decisions had made the situation more difficult. They now download offline maps, carry a basic paper map, check the weather more carefully, and share their route with someone before leaving. Looking back, the writer says the frightening part was not being lost for a short time but realising how quickly confidence can disappear when familiar technology stops working. The story has become one they often tell because it is both embarrassing and useful: adventure is more enjoyable when preparation gives you enough flexibility to deal with the unexpected.","audioScript":"Traveller: We were supposed to have a two-hour layover, but the first flight arrived late and we missed the connection. Friend: So what did you do? Traveller: At first I panicked because the next direct flight was the following morning. Then an airline employee suggested a different route with two shorter flights. Friend: Did you take it? Traveller: Yes, although our luggage didn't arrive with us. We reached the city almost eight hours late, exhausted but relieved. Friend: Would you do anything differently now? Traveller: Definitely. I keep essential medicine and one change of clothes in my hand luggage, and I leave more time between connecting flights. It was frustrating, but it taught me how to stay calm when a plan changes completely.","questions":[{"q":"What made the hikers realise their preparation had been weak?","options":["The fog and loss of reliable phone navigation","The cost of accommodation","A delayed flight"],"answer":"The fog and loss of reliable phone navigation","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"Which preparation change do they now make before leaving?","options":["They download offline maps and share their route","They travel without telling anyone","They rely only on live phone data"],"answer":"They download offline maps and share their route","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"What does the writer identify as the most frightening part?","options":["How quickly confidence disappeared when familiar technology failed","The price of the trip","Meeting other hikers"],"answer":"How quickly confidence disappeared when familiar technology failed","tag":"reading:inference"},{"q":"Why has the experience become a story they often retell?","options":["It is both entertaining and useful as a lesson about preparation","It proves travel should be avoided","It was completely successful from the start"],"answer":"It is both entertaining and useful as a lesson about preparation","tag":"reading:purpose"},{"q":"Why did the traveller miss the connection?","options":["The first flight arrived late","They forgot their passport","The airport closed"],"answer":"The first flight arrived late","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What alternative did the airline employee suggest?","options":["A route using two shorter flights","Waiting a full week","Travelling only by bus"],"answer":"A route using two shorter flights","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What problem remained after they reached the city?","options":["Their luggage had not arrived","They had no accommodation","They lost their tickets"],"answer":"Their luggage had not arrived","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What practical lesson did the traveller learn?","options":["Carry essentials in hand luggage and allow more connection time","Never travel by plane again","Always choose the cheapest route"],"answer":"Carry essentials in hand luggage and allow more connection time","tag":"listening:inference"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Hi everyone,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Hi everyone,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to whatsapp travel update","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to whatsapp travel update","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “connected with a nearby place or community”?","options":["local","journey","route"],"answer":"local","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Thanks — I look forward to hearing from you.","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Thanks — I look forward to hearing from you.","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Send a 70–90 word WhatsApp message explaining what went wrong on a journey and how it ended.","minWords":70,"maxWords":90,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle narrative tenses so learners can tell a clear sequence of past events.","b2Lift":"Create suspense, evaluate events, manage listener interest, and shift naturally between background, main events, and reflection.","performance":"Tell an extended travel story with clear sequencing, evaluation, and spontaneous responses to listener questions.","pronunciation":"Use narrative stress and pausing to highlight turning points and unexpected events.","mediation":"Retell a partner's travel story to another group while preserving the key sequence and outcome.","functions":["narrate an extended experience","highlight a turning point","evaluate what was learned"],"discourse":["setting the scene","signalling an unexpected change","closing with reflection"],"chunks":["miss a connection","take a wrong turn","off the beaten track","a close call","turn out differently"],"interactionExpressions":["You won't believe what happened next.","What happened after that?","That must have been stressful."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Choose the best sequence: “We ___ for three hours when the fog suddenly ___ in.”","options":["had been hiking / rolled","were hiking / had rolled","hiked / was rolling"],"answer":"had been hiking / rolled","tag":"grammar:sequence"},{"q":"Which sentence gives background action interrupted by a main event?","options":["We were checking the map when the rain started.","We had checked the map when the rain had started.","We checked the map while the rain had started."],"answer":"We were checking the map when the rain started.","tag":"grammar:background"},{"q":"Which sentence makes it clear the wrong turn happened before they noticed the problem?","options":["We had taken a wrong turn before we realised it.","We were taking a wrong turn before we had realised it.","We took a wrong turn after we had realised it."],"answer":"We had taken a wrong turn before we realised it.","tag":"grammar:prior-event"},{"q":"Choose the most natural narrative sentence.","options":["By the time help arrived, it had already become dark.","By the time help had arrived, it already became dark.","By the time help arrives, it had become dark."],"answer":"By the time help arrived, it had already become dark.","tag":"grammar:time-clause"},{"q":"Which option best creates a clear turning point in a story?","options":["We were discussing what to do when a farmer appeared.","We discussed what to do when a farmer had been appearing.","We had discussed what to do when a farmer was appeared."],"answer":"We were discussing what to do when a farmer appeared.","tag":"grammar:narrative"},{"q":"Complete: “I ___ my passport twice before I finally ___ the hotel.”","options":["had checked / left","was checking / had left","checked / had been leaving"],"answer":"had checked / left","tag":"grammar:sequence"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l4","number":4,"title":"Technology & Social Media","outcome":"Debate whether social media does more harm than good and reach a reasoned position after responding to opposing views.","grammarFocus":"Present Perfect Continuous","grammarRule":"Use Present Perfect Continuous accurately for facebook/x post about screen time. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Facebook/X post about screen time","vocabulary":["platform","privacy","algorithm","notification","screen time","distraction"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “platform” to its meaning.","options":["an online service where people communicate or share content","a set of rules a computer follows to make decisions","time spent using screens"],"answer":"an online service where people communicate or share content","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “privacy” to its meaning.","options":["control over personal information","message about something new","something that takes attention away from a task"],"answer":"control over personal information","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “algorithm” to its meaning.","options":["a set of rules a computer follows to make decisions","time spent using screens","an online service where people communicate or share content"],"answer":"a set of rules a computer follows to make decisions","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “notification” to its meaning.","options":["message about something new","something that takes attention away from a task","control over personal information"],"answer":"message about something new","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “screen time” to its meaning.","options":["time spent using screens","an online service where people communicate or share content","a set of rules a computer follows to make decisions"],"answer":"time spent using screens","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “distraction” to its meaning.","options":["something that takes attention away from a task","control over personal information","message about something new"],"answer":"something that takes attention away from a task","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “control over personal information”?","options":["privacy","notification","distraction"],"answer":"privacy","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: time spent using screens?","options":["screen time","platform","algorithm"],"answer":"screen time","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “an online service where people communicate or share content”.","answer":"platform","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “I turned off every ___ so I could focus.”","options":["notification","platform","privacy"],"answer":"notification","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"Yusuf has been working from his laptop for eight hours a day for the past three years. Recently, he has been feeling more anxious and less able to concentrate. Last month, he decided to try a digital detox — no social media for two weeks. He has been sleeping better and has been spending more time with his family since he started. The first week was harder than Yusuf expected. He kept reaching for his phone without thinking, especially when a task became difficult. By the second week, however, he had started noticing the difference between using technology deliberately and using it automatically. He still depended on messaging apps for work and family communication, but he stopped keeping every notification active. He also began leaving his phone in another room during focused work. Yusuf does not believe technology itself is the problem. In his view, the more important question is whether users design their habits or allow platforms to design those habits for them. His experience has made him less interested in dramatic claims that social media is either completely harmful or completely beneficial.","audioScript":"Moderator: Do you think social media is damaging young people's attention spans? Speaker 1: It can be, especially when platforms are designed to keep people scrolling. I've been noticing that even adults struggle to focus after checking short videos repeatedly. Speaker 2: I agree that distraction is real, but social media also gives learners access to communities and information they would never find locally. Moderator: So where is the balance? Speaker 1: I would focus on habits rather than banning platforms. Turn off unnecessary notifications, set time limits, and decide what you are opening an app to do. Speaker 2: Exactly. The technology is a tool, but people need enough awareness to use it deliberately.","questions":[{"q":"What habit did Yusuf notice during his digital detox?","options":["He reached for his phone automatically when work became difficult","He never used messaging apps","He stopped using technology for work"],"answer":"He reached for his phone automatically when work became difficult","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"What change helped him protect focused work?","options":["Leaving his phone in another room","Turning on more notifications","Checking short videos more often"],"answer":"Leaving his phone in another room","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"What distinction became important to Yusuf?","options":["Using technology deliberately versus automatically","Work technology versus entertainment technology only","New phones versus old phones"],"answer":"Using technology deliberately versus automatically","tag":"reading:main-idea"},{"q":"Why does Yusuf reject extreme claims about social media?","options":["He thinks outcomes depend partly on how people design their habits","He believes every platform is harmless","He thinks all technology should be banned"],"answer":"He thinks outcomes depend partly on how people design their habits","tag":"reading:stance"},{"q":"What risk does Speaker 1 associate with short-video use?","options":["Reduced ability to focus","Loss of all social contact","Higher travel costs"],"answer":"Reduced ability to focus","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What benefit does Speaker 2 identify?","options":["Access to communities and information","Fewer learning opportunities","Less communication"],"answer":"Access to communities and information","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What does Speaker 1 recommend instead of banning platforms?","options":["Changing habits such as notifications and time limits","Using every app continuously","Removing all educational technology"],"answer":"Changing habits such as notifications and time limits","tag":"listening:response"},{"q":"What solution do both speakers broadly support?","options":["Managing habits deliberately rather than simply banning platforms","Removing all technology from education","Keeping every notification active"],"answer":"Managing habits deliberately rather than simply banning platforms","tag":"listening:synthesis"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Here is something worth discussing:","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Here is something worth discussing:","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to facebook/x post about screen time","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to facebook/x post about screen time","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “something that takes attention away from a task”?","options":["distraction","privacy","notification"],"answer":"distraction","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["What do you think?","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"What do you think?","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write a 70–100 word Facebook/X post about screen time and give one practical suggestion.","minWords":70,"maxWords":100,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle Present Perfect Continuous to describe ongoing habits and recent change.","b2Lift":"Express stance, concede a point, challenge claims politely, use evidence, and distinguish certainty from personal impression.","performance":"Debate whether social media does more harm than good and reach a reasoned position after responding to opposing views.","pronunciation":"Use contrastive stress to show which part of an argument you accept, reject, or qualify.","mediation":"Summarise the strongest argument from the opposite side before giving your own response.","functions":["describe ongoing change","express and qualify an opinion","respond to a counterargument"],"discourse":["conceding before disagreeing","supporting a claim with evidence","summarising a position"],"chunks":["digital distraction","online privacy","attention span","algorithmic feed","set healthy boundaries"],"interactionExpressions":["To some extent, I agree, but...","The evidence seems to suggest...","On balance, I would say..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Which sentence best emphasises the duration of an activity that is still continuing?","options":["I've been reducing my screen time for three weeks.","I've reduced my screen time for three weeks yesterday.","I reduced my screen time since three weeks."],"answer":"I've been reducing my screen time for three weeks.","tag":"grammar:duration"},{"q":"Choose the best sentence for a recent repeated activity with a present effect.","options":["She's been checking notifications constantly, so she's finding it hard to focus.","She checked notifications constantly since Monday, so she's finding it hard to focus.","She has checked notifications yesterday, so she's finding it hard to focus."],"answer":"She's been checking notifications constantly, so she's finding it hard to focus.","tag":"grammar:present-result"},{"q":"Which question asks about the length of an ongoing habit?","options":["How long have you been using that app?","How long did you use that app since January?","How long are you use that app?"],"answer":"How long have you been using that app?","tag":"grammar:function"},{"q":"Choose the best contrast.","options":["I've read the report, and I've been thinking about its argument all morning.","I've been reading the report completely, and I thought about it since morning.","I read the report since morning, and I've think about it."],"answer":"I've read the report, and I've been thinking about its argument all morning.","tag":"grammar:result-vs-duration"},{"q":"The action is temporary and ongoing. Choose the best form: “This month, I ___ social media only after work.”","options":["have been using","used","use permanently"],"answer":"have been using","tag":"grammar:temporary"},{"q":"Which sentence focuses more on the activity than the completed result?","options":["We've been discussing the policy for an hour.","We've discussed the policy and reached a final decision.","We discussed the policy last Friday."],"answer":"We've been discussing the policy for an hour.","tag":"grammar:focus"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l5","number":5,"title":"Health & Wellbeing","outcome":"Evaluate several wellbeing options with a partner and agree on a realistic plan while explaining priorities and possible obstacles.","grammarFocus":"Should / must / have to","grammarRule":"Use Should / must / have to accurately for supportive whatsapp advice. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Supportive WhatsApp advice","vocabulary":["burnout","wellbeing","stress","routine","recovery","balance"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “burnout” to its meaning.","options":["extreme tiredness caused by long-term stress","pressure or worry","the process of becoming well or strong again"],"answer":"extreme tiredness caused by long-term stress","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “wellbeing” to its meaning.","options":["the state of being healthy and comfortable","regular way of doing something","a healthy division of time or attention"],"answer":"the state of being healthy and comfortable","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “stress” to its meaning.","options":["pressure or worry","the process of becoming well or strong again","extreme tiredness caused by long-term stress"],"answer":"pressure or worry","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “routine” to its meaning.","options":["regular way of doing something","a healthy division of time or attention","the state of being healthy and comfortable"],"answer":"regular way of doing something","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “recovery” to its meaning.","options":["the process of becoming well or strong again","extreme tiredness caused by long-term stress","pressure or worry"],"answer":"the process of becoming well or strong again","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “balance” to its meaning.","options":["a healthy division of time or attention","the state of being healthy and comfortable","regular way of doing something"],"answer":"a healthy division of time or attention","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “the state of being healthy and comfortable”?","options":["wellbeing","routine","balance"],"answer":"wellbeing","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: the process of becoming well or strong again?","options":["recovery","burnout","stress"],"answer":"recovery","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “extreme tiredness caused by long-term stress”.","answer":"burnout","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “Months of overwork can lead to ___.”","options":["burnout","wellbeing","stress"],"answer":"burnout","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"Fardowsa used to work sixty hours a week and rarely took breaks. She often felt exhausted but kept pushing through. After she started experiencing constant headaches, her doctor told her she had to make changes. She now takes short walks during the day, and she has started saying no to extra tasks. She says she should have made these changes years earlier. Fardowsa's first response was to make an ambitious plan: exercise every morning, stop drinking coffee, cook every meal at home, and sleep eight hours every night. After three days, the plan collapsed because it did not fit her working life. Her doctor then suggested choosing two changes that were small enough to continue during busy weeks. Fardowsa began taking a twenty-minute walk after lunch and protecting a fixed bedtime on four nights each week. The changes were less impressive on paper, but they were sustainable. She now believes that wellbeing advice can fail when it ignores a person's real responsibilities. A useful plan, she says, should reduce risk without making people feel that one difficult week means they have failed completely.","audioScript":"Coach: You said you're exhausted most afternoons. What does a normal day look like? Client: I start work at seven, often skip lunch, and then study online until about eleven. Coach: That's a demanding schedule. You probably can't change everything at once. Client: That's the problem. Every plan I make lasts three days. Coach: Then let's choose one change with the biggest effect. You might want to protect your sleep first and add a short break before your evening study. Client: I could manage that. Exercise every day would be harder. Coach: Good. The goal is not a perfect routine. It's a routine you can still follow during a busy week.","questions":[{"q":"Why did Fardowsa's first wellbeing plan fail?","options":["It tried to change too many things at once","It contained no changes","Her doctor told her to stop sleeping"],"answer":"It tried to change too many things at once","tag":"reading:reason"},{"q":"Which two changes did she eventually choose?","options":["A short walk after lunch and a protected bedtime on four nights","Daily intense exercise and no food after lunch","Leaving her job and stopping study"],"answer":"A short walk after lunch and a protected bedtime on four nights","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"Why were the smaller changes more successful?","options":["They were sustainable during busy weeks","They produced instant results","They required no effort"],"answer":"They were sustainable during busy weeks","tag":"reading:inference"},{"q":"What broader lesson does Fardowsa draw from the experience?","options":["Advice must fit a person's real responsibilities","A good plan should always be dramatic","One difficult week proves a plan has failed"],"answer":"Advice must fit a person's real responsibilities","tag":"reading:main-idea"},{"q":"Which habit does the client describe?","options":["Skipping lunch and studying late","Sleeping ten hours every night","Finishing work at midday"],"answer":"Skipping lunch and studying late","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"Why does the coach avoid changing everything at once?","options":["The client's previous plans were not sustainable","The client has no wellbeing problem","Small changes never work"],"answer":"The client's previous plans were not sustainable","tag":"listening:reason"},{"q":"Which area does the coach prioritise first?","options":["Protecting sleep and adding a short break","Buying new exercise equipment","Changing jobs immediately"],"answer":"Protecting sleep and adding a short break","tag":"listening:priority"},{"q":"Why does the coach recommend starting with only one or two changes?","options":["A sustainable plan is more useful than an unrealistic perfect routine","Exercise is never useful","The client has no need to change"],"answer":"A sustainable plan is more useful than an unrealistic perfect routine","tag":"listening:reasoning"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Hi everyone,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Hi everyone,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to supportive whatsapp advice","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to supportive whatsapp advice","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “a healthy division of time or attention”?","options":["balance","wellbeing","routine"],"answer":"balance","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Thanks — I look forward to hearing from you.","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Thanks — I look forward to hearing from you.","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Reply to a stressed friend on WhatsApp in 70–90 words with practical, non-medical advice.","minWords":70,"maxWords":90,"humanGraded":true,"checkpoint":true},"foundation":"Recycle should, must, and have to for familiar advice and obligation.","b2Lift":"Give diplomatic advice, compare interventions, explain risk and cause, and negotiate realistic behavioural change.","performance":"Evaluate several wellbeing options with a partner and agree on a realistic plan while explaining priorities and possible obstacles.","pronunciation":"Use softer intonation for sensitive advice and stronger stress only when expressing genuine obligation.","mediation":"Listen to a person's concerns, identify the main problem, and explain the agreed plan back to them clearly.","functions":["give sensitive advice","compare possible solutions","negotiate a realistic plan"],"discourse":["identifying the main issue","ranking options","agreeing a practical next step"],"chunks":["work-life balance","warning signs","sustainable routine","reduce the risk","make a gradual change"],"interactionExpressions":["You might want to consider...","One realistic option would be...","The main concern seems to be..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"A friend is exhausted but not in immediate danger. Which advice sounds most appropriately diplomatic?","options":["You should probably protect your sleep before changing everything else.","You must sleep exactly eight hours tonight.","You have to quit your job immediately."],"answer":"You should probably protect your sleep before changing everything else.","tag":"grammar:strength"},{"q":"Which sentence expresses an external obligation rather than personal advice?","options":["Employees have to wear protective equipment in this area.","Employees should wear protective equipment because I think it looks better.","Employees might want protective equipment if they feel like it."],"answer":"Employees have to wear protective equipment in this area.","tag":"grammar:obligation"},{"q":"Which sentence expresses a strong rule from the speaker?","options":["You must not share a patient's private information.","You don't have to share a patient's private information.","You should not necessarily share a patient's private information."],"answer":"You must not share a patient's private information.","tag":"grammar:prohibition"},{"q":"What does “You don't have to attend if you're ill” mean?","options":["Attendance is not necessary.","Attendance is forbidden.","Attendance is strongly recommended."],"answer":"Attendance is not necessary.","tag":"grammar:meaning"},{"q":"Choose the best recommendation when several options are possible.","options":["You might want to start with one change you can maintain.","You must choose the exact plan I prefer.","You have to follow every possible suggestion."],"answer":"You might want to start with one change you can maintain.","tag":"grammar:pragmatics"},{"q":"Complete the contrast: “I ___ submit the report today because it's a company deadline, but I ___ answer every non-urgent message tonight.”","options":["have to / don't have to","should / mustn't","mustn't / should"],"answer":"have to / don't have to","tag":"grammar:contrast"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l6","number":6,"title":"Food & Culture","outcome":"Present a culturally meaningful dish, compare it fairly with another tradition, and defend a recommendation in response to questions.","grammarFocus":"Comparatives / Superlatives / Quantifiers","grammarRule":"Use Comparatives / Superlatives / Quantifiers accurately for food recommendation message. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Food recommendation message","vocabulary":["ingredient","flavour","recipe","dish","spice","portion"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “ingredient” to its meaning.","options":["food used to make a dish","instructions for making food","a plant substance used to add flavour to food"],"answer":"food used to make a dish","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “flavour” to its meaning.","options":["taste of food or drink","food prepared and served as part of a meal","amount served to one person"],"answer":"taste of food or drink","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “recipe” to its meaning.","options":["instructions for making food","a plant substance used to add flavour to food","food used to make a dish"],"answer":"instructions for making food","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “dish” to its meaning.","options":["food prepared and served as part of a meal","amount served to one person","taste of food or drink"],"answer":"food prepared and served as part of a meal","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “spice” to its meaning.","options":["a plant substance used to add flavour to food","food used to make a dish","instructions for making food"],"answer":"a plant substance used to add flavour to food","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “portion” to its meaning.","options":["amount served to one person","taste of food or drink","food prepared and served as part of a meal"],"answer":"amount served to one person","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “taste of food or drink”?","options":["flavour","dish","portion"],"answer":"flavour","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: a plant substance used to add flavour to food?","options":["spice","ingredient","recipe"],"answer":"spice","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “food used to make a dish”.","answer":"ingredient","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “Cardamom is a key ___ in the recipe.”","options":["ingredient","flavour","recipe"],"answer":"ingredient","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"In Hargeisa, Deeqa's grandmother taught her to cook the slowest, richest stews she has ever tasted, using far fewer spices than most restaurant food today. When Deeqa moved to Nairobi for university, she discovered that Kenyan food was spicier and used a much wider variety of vegetables. She now blends both traditions, saying her cooking has become far more interesting than either style alone. As Deeqa became more confident, she also became more careful about the way she compared cuisines. She used to describe one dish as 'better' or 'more authentic' without explaining what she meant. Now she talks about texture, preparation, ingredients, occasion, and personal memory. A dish may be lighter but more aromatic, or less spicy but richer because it is cooked slowly. She has also noticed that recipes change when families move. Ingredients may be unavailable, younger cooks may reduce preparation time, and local tastes influence the final result. Deeqa does not see these changes as proof that a tradition has disappeared. Instead, she thinks food shows how culture can remain recognisable while adapting to a new place.","audioScript":"Host: If someone had never tried this dish before, how would you describe it? Cook: It's a slow-cooked stew with a deep flavour, but it isn't especially spicy. The texture is softer than many restaurant versions because we cook it for longer. Host: Has the recipe changed in your family? Cook: A little. My grandmother used ingredients that are difficult to find here, so we substitute two of them. Host: Does that make it less traditional? Cook: I don't think so. The method and the occasion still matter. We usually prepare it when the extended family meets. For me, the dish is not only about taste; it carries memories of people and places.","questions":[{"q":"How has Deeqa's way of comparing cuisines changed?","options":["She now explains texture, preparation, ingredients, occasion, and memory","She simply calls one dish better","She avoids comparing food at all"],"answer":"She now explains texture, preparation, ingredients, occasion, and memory","tag":"reading:development"},{"q":"Why may recipes change when families move?","options":["Ingredients, time, and local tastes can differ","Traditions always disappear abroad","Families stop cooking"],"answer":"Ingredients, time, and local tastes can differ","tag":"reading:reason"},{"q":"What does Deeqa think adaptation can show?","options":["A culture can remain recognisable while changing","Only unchanged recipes are cultural","Food has no connection to identity"],"answer":"A culture can remain recognisable while changing","tag":"reading:stance"},{"q":"Which comparison is more nuanced according to the text?","options":["Explaining how a dish differs in texture and flavour","Saying one dish is simply better","Ranking food without criteria"],"answer":"Explaining how a dish differs in texture and flavour","tag":"reading:inference"},{"q":"How does the cook describe the dish?","options":["Slow-cooked, deep in flavour, and not especially spicy","Very sweet and uncooked","Extremely spicy and fast-cooked"],"answer":"Slow-cooked, deep in flavour, and not especially spicy","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"Why has the family substituted some ingredients?","options":["Some original ingredients are difficult to find","They want to remove all tradition","The recipe never used ingredients"],"answer":"Some original ingredients are difficult to find","tag":"listening:reason"},{"q":"What makes the dish culturally important to the cook?","options":["It is connected to family gatherings and memories","It is the cheapest meal","It is served only to tourists"],"answer":"It is connected to family gatherings and memories","tag":"listening:inference"},{"q":"Why does the cook still consider the adapted recipe traditional?","options":["The method and cultural occasion are still preserved","Every ingredient is exactly the same","It is served only in restaurants"],"answer":"The method and cultural occasion are still preserved","tag":"listening:interpretation"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Hi everyone,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Hi everyone,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to food recommendation message","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to food recommendation message","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “amount served to one person”?","options":["portion","flavour","dish"],"answer":"portion","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Thanks — I look forward to hearing from you.","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Thanks — I look forward to hearing from you.","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write an 80–100 word food recommendation to an international visitor.","minWords":80,"maxWords":100,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle comparative, superlative, and quantifier forms for familiar descriptions.","b2Lift":"Make nuanced comparisons, qualify claims, describe sensory experience precisely, and connect food choices to culture and identity.","performance":"Present a culturally meaningful dish, compare it fairly with another tradition, and defend a recommendation in response to questions.","pronunciation":"Use descriptive stress to foreground the qualities that make two foods meaningfully different.","mediation":"Explain a food tradition to someone unfamiliar with it without assuming shared cultural knowledge.","functions":["compare traditions","describe taste and texture precisely","explain cultural significance"],"discourse":["qualifying a comparison","moving from description to cultural meaning","responding to a challenge"],"chunks":["a staple ingredient","rich in flavour","an acquired taste","cultural significance","passed down through generations"],"interactionExpressions":["What makes it distinctive is...","Compared with..., it tends to...","For someone trying it for the first time, I'd suggest..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Choose the most precise comparison.","options":["This version is slightly less spicy but considerably richer in flavour.","This version is the less spicy and much richer than all.","This version has fewer spicy and most rich flavour."],"answer":"This version is slightly less spicy but considerably richer in flavour.","tag":"grammar:modified-comparison"},{"q":"Which sentence correctly compares two dishes using a countable noun?","options":["This dish contains fewer vegetables than the other one.","This dish contains less vegetables than the other one.","This dish contains the fewest vegetables than the other one."],"answer":"This dish contains fewer vegetables than the other one.","tag":"grammar:quantifier"},{"q":"Which sentence uses a non-count quantifier correctly?","options":["There's far too much salt in this sauce.","There are far too many salt in this sauce.","There's far too few salt in this sauce."],"answer":"There's far too much salt in this sauce.","tag":"grammar:quantifier"},{"q":"Choose the best sentence for a strong but qualified comparison.","options":["The market version is by far the most aromatic of the three.","The market version is very more aromatic of the three.","The market version is by far more aromatic than all three."],"answer":"The market version is by far the most aromatic of the three.","tag":"grammar:superlative"},{"q":"Which option best expresses similarity rather than superiority?","options":["The two dishes are almost as rich as each other.","The two dishes are almost richer as each other.","The two dishes are the most equally rich."],"answer":"The two dishes are almost as rich as each other.","tag":"grammar:comparison"},{"q":"Complete: “The home version uses ___ oil, but it takes ___ longer to prepare.”","options":["less / much","fewer / many","least / more"],"answer":"less / much","tag":"grammar:contrast"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l7","number":7,"title":"Education & Learning","outcome":"Debate one change schools should make and defend the proposal with evidence, comparison, and a response to objections.","grammarFocus":"Used to / Would","grammarRule":"Use Used to / Would accurately for online discussion post about education. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Online discussion post about education","vocabulary":["curriculum","assessment","feedback","method","independent","progress"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “curriculum” to its meaning.","options":["the subjects and content taught in a course","comments that help improvement","able to work or decide without help"],"answer":"the subjects and content taught in a course","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “assessment” to its meaning.","options":["a task used to measure knowledge or ability","a particular way of doing something","improvement over time"],"answer":"a task used to measure knowledge or ability","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “feedback” to its meaning.","options":["comments that help improvement","able to work or decide without help","the subjects and content taught in a course"],"answer":"comments that help improvement","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “method” to its meaning.","options":["a particular way of doing something","improvement over time","a task used to measure knowledge or ability"],"answer":"a particular way of doing something","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “independent” to its meaning.","options":["able to work or decide without help","the subjects and content taught in a course","comments that help improvement"],"answer":"able to work or decide without help","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “progress” to its meaning.","options":["improvement over time","a task used to measure knowledge or ability","a particular way of doing something"],"answer":"improvement over time","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “a task used to measure knowledge or ability”?","options":["assessment","method","progress"],"answer":"assessment","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: able to work or decide without help?","options":["independent","curriculum","feedback"],"answer":"independent","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “the subjects and content taught in a course”.","answer":"curriculum","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “She won a ___ to study engineering.”","options":["scholarship","curriculum","assessment"],"answer":"scholarship","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"When Hodan was in school, students used to sit in rows and copy notes from the blackboard. Teachers would rarely use group work. Today, Hodan teaches at the same school, but classes now use tablets, and students would often work together on projects instead of memorising facts alone. She believes today's students think more independently than her generation did. Hodan has also become more cautious about saying that modern education is automatically better. Digital resources give students faster access to information, but access does not guarantee understanding. Some learners become distracted, and others copy answers without processing them. At the same time, she remembers that the older system often rewarded memorising information without asking students to apply it. Her current classes combine short explanations, individual preparation, pair work, and tasks where students must use what they have learned to solve a problem. She believes the strongest education systems do not simply replace old methods with new technology. They decide which practices actually help learners think, remember, communicate, and become more independent.","audioScript":"Teacher 1: When I was at school, we would copy pages of notes and memorise them for the exam. Teacher 2: We did the same, but I don't think everything about that system was useless. We learned to concentrate for long periods. Teacher 1: True, although we rarely had to explain our own thinking. Teacher 2: That's what I value about project work now. Students have to apply information, not just repeat it. Teacher 1: The difficulty is balance. Too much group work can also hide individual weaknesses. Teacher 2: Exactly. I would keep explicit teaching, but I would combine it with tasks where every learner has to produce something independently.","questions":[{"q":"Why is Hodan cautious about saying modern education is automatically better?","options":["Digital access can help but does not guarantee understanding","Technology has no educational value","Old education had no weaknesses"],"answer":"Digital access can help but does not guarantee understanding","tag":"reading:stance"},{"q":"What weakness does she identify in the older system?","options":["It often rewarded memorisation without application","Students had too much project work","Teachers used too much technology"],"answer":"It often rewarded memorisation without application","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"What combination does Hodan use in her current classes?","options":["Explanation, preparation, pair work, and applied tasks","Only individual memorisation","Only digital videos"],"answer":"Explanation, preparation, pair work, and applied tasks","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"What principle does she use to judge teaching methods?","options":["Whether they help learners think, remember, communicate, and become independent","Whether they are new","Whether they use the most devices"],"answer":"Whether they help learners think, remember, communicate, and become independent","tag":"reading:main-idea"},{"q":"What did Teacher 1 often do at school?","options":["Copy and memorise notes","Lead project teams","Use online simulations"],"answer":"Copy and memorise notes","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What benefit does Teacher 2 still associate with the old system?","options":["Long periods of concentration","Constant collaboration","Automatic creativity"],"answer":"Long periods of concentration","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What concern do they express about too much group work?","options":["It can hide individual weaknesses","It prevents all communication","It makes exams impossible"],"answer":"It can hide individual weaknesses","tag":"listening:inference"},{"q":"What position do the two teachers eventually share?","options":["A balanced combination of explicit teaching and active application is preferable","Old methods should return unchanged","Group work should replace all individual work"],"answer":"A balanced combination of explicit teaching and active application is preferable","tag":"listening:synthesis"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Hello,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Hello,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to online discussion post about education","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to online discussion post about education","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “improvement over time”?","options":["progress","assessment","method"],"answer":"progress","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Thank you.","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Thank you.","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write a 90–120 word online discussion response: Was education better in the past?","minWords":90,"maxWords":120,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle used to and would to describe familiar past habits.","b2Lift":"Evaluate educational practices, compare systems, support claims with examples, and explain how learning strategies affect outcomes.","performance":"Debate one change schools should make and defend the proposal with evidence, comparison, and a response to objections.","pronunciation":"Use connected speech in familiar past-habit phrases and clear prominence when contrasting past and present.","mediation":"Explain an older person's educational experience to younger learners and identify what remains useful today.","functions":["compare past and present systems","evaluate a learning method","recommend educational change"],"discourse":["contrasting periods","giving evidence for evaluation","acknowledging an opposing view"],"chunks":["hands-on learning","independent study","learning outcome","rote memorisation","apply knowledge"],"interactionExpressions":["One advantage of that approach is...","That may be true, although...","The change I would prioritise is..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Which sentence describes a past state that is no longer true?","options":["I used to be nervous before presentations.","I would be nervous as a permanent state when I was younger.","I use to be nervous before presentations."],"answer":"I used to be nervous before presentations.","tag":"grammar:past-state"},{"q":"Which sentence naturally describes a repeated past action?","options":["Our teacher would give us a short quiz every Friday.","Our teacher would know all the answers every Friday.","Our teacher used giving us a short quiz every Friday."],"answer":"Our teacher would give us a short quiz every Friday.","tag":"grammar:past-habit"},{"q":"Why is “would” less suitable than “used to” in “I ___ hate maths”?","options":["“Hate” describes a state, not a repeated action.","“Would” can never refer to the past.","“Used to” is only for future habits."],"answer":"“Hate” describes a state, not a repeated action.","tag":"grammar:meaning"},{"q":"Choose the best contrast between past and present learning.","options":["We used to copy notes, but now we spend more time applying ideas.","We would copy notes, but now we used to apply ideas.","We used to copying notes, but now we would apply ideas."],"answer":"We used to copy notes, but now we spend more time applying ideas.","tag":"grammar:contrast"},{"q":"Which question most naturally asks about a repeated childhood routine?","options":["What would you usually do after school?","What would you be after school?","What used you to doing after school?"],"answer":"What would you usually do after school?","tag":"grammar:function"},{"q":"Complete: “There ___ be a computer room here, and students ___ line up to use it.”","options":["used to / would","would / used to","used / would to"],"answer":"used to / would","tag":"grammar:contrast"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l8","number":8,"title":"Money & Business","outcome":"Pitch a small business, answer challenging questions, and defend financial decisions by comparing risks, benefits, and alternative scenarios.","grammarFocus":"First / Second Conditionals","grammarRule":"Use First / Second Conditionals accurately for short business pitch message. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Short business pitch message","vocabulary":["customer","profit","capital","competitor","service","risk"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “customer” to its meaning.","options":["person who buys a product or service","money used to start or grow a business","work done to help or provide something for a customer"],"answer":"person who buys a product or service","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “profit” to its meaning.","options":["money left after costs","a person or business offering a similar product","the possibility that something harmful or unwanted may happen"],"answer":"money left after costs","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “capital” to its meaning.","options":["money used to start or grow a business","work done to help or provide something for a customer","person who buys a product or service"],"answer":"money used to start or grow a business","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “competitor” to its meaning.","options":["a person or business offering a similar product","the possibility that something harmful or unwanted may happen","money left after costs"],"answer":"a person or business offering a similar product","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “service” to its meaning.","options":["work done to help or provide something for a customer","person who buys a product or service","money used to start or grow a business"],"answer":"work done to help or provide something for a customer","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “risk” to its meaning.","options":["the possibility that something harmful or unwanted may happen","money left after costs","a person or business offering a similar product"],"answer":"the possibility that something harmful or unwanted may happen","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “money left after costs”?","options":["profit","competitor","risk"],"answer":"profit","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: work done to help or provide something for a customer?","options":["service","customer","capital"],"answer":"service","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “person who buys a product or service”.","answer":"customer","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “Every new business involves some ___.”","options":["risk","customer","profit"],"answer":"risk","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"If Khadar hadn't taken a small loan five years ago, he wouldn't have opened his electronics shop. It was a risk — if the shop fails, he'll lose his savings. But if it continues to grow like this, he'll be able to open a second branch next year. He says that if he had waited for the 'perfect moment', he would probably still be waiting today. Khadar's first year was more difficult than the short success stories he had read online. Sales were unpredictable, and some customers wanted credit even when his cash flow was already tight. He learned to separate revenue from profit and to keep enough money available for restocking. When a friend suggested opening a second branch immediately, Khadar refused. He calculated that expansion would increase rent, staffing costs, and inventory before the first shop had produced stable monthly profit. Instead, he invested in a better ordering system and negotiated with suppliers. The decision was less exciting than opening a new branch, but it reduced waste and improved margins. Khadar now describes entrepreneurship as a series of calculated choices rather than one dramatic act of courage.","audioScript":"Investor: Your idea sounds interesting, but what happens if customer numbers are lower than you expect? Founder: If demand is weak in the first three months, we'll reduce opening hours and delay hiring a second employee. Investor: And if the concept works quickly? Founder: I still wouldn't expand immediately. If we had more cash, expansion would be tempting, but I would first confirm that repeat customers are increasing. Investor: What is your biggest cost risk? Founder: Imported equipment. A currency change could raise the price significantly, so we've identified a local alternative. Investor: That makes the plan more credible. You're not assuming that everything will go perfectly.","questions":[{"q":"Why did Khadar refuse to open a second branch immediately?","options":["The first shop had not yet produced stable monthly profit","He wanted to close the first shop","Customers had stopped buying completely"],"answer":"The first shop had not yet produced stable monthly profit","tag":"reading:reason"},{"q":"What did he do instead of expanding?","options":["Improved ordering and negotiated with suppliers","Borrowed money for a larger building","Stopped tracking stock"],"answer":"Improved ordering and negotiated with suppliers","tag":"reading:response"},{"q":"What distinction did Khadar learn to make?","options":["Revenue is not the same as profit","Risk is always negative","Expansion always increases profit"],"answer":"Revenue is not the same as profit","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"How does Khadar now describe entrepreneurship?","options":["A series of calculated choices","One dramatic act of courage","A way to avoid planning"],"answer":"A series of calculated choices","tag":"reading:stance"},{"q":"What will the founder do if demand is weak?","options":["Reduce opening hours and delay hiring","Expand immediately","Ignore customer numbers"],"answer":"Reduce opening hours and delay hiring","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"Why would the founder avoid immediate expansion even if the idea works?","options":["They want evidence of repeat demand first","Expansion is illegal","The investor forbids growth"],"answer":"They want evidence of repeat demand first","tag":"listening:reason"},{"q":"What risk is connected to imported equipment?","options":["Currency changes may raise the cost","The equipment has no price","Local alternatives do not exist"],"answer":"Currency changes may raise the cost","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"Why does the investor find the plan more credible?","options":["The founder has considered risks and fallback options","The founder promises immediate expansion","The founder ignores currency changes"],"answer":"The founder has considered risks and fallback options","tag":"listening:evaluation"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Hi everyone,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Hi everyone,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to short business pitch message","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to short business pitch message","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “the possibility that something harmful or unwanted may happen”?","options":["risk","profit","competitor"],"answer":"risk","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Thanks — I look forward to hearing from you.","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Thanks — I look forward to hearing from you.","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write an 80–100 word business pitch message explaining a problem, solution and customer.","minWords":90,"maxWords":110,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle first and second conditionals to discuss likely and hypothetical business outcomes.","b2Lift":"Evaluate risk, compare trade-offs, persuade an audience, respond to objections, and make conditional projections with appropriate certainty.","performance":"Pitch a small business, answer challenging questions, and defend financial decisions by comparing risks, benefits, and alternative scenarios.","pronunciation":"Use persuasive emphasis on key benefits while lowering prominence on supporting detail.","mediation":"Summarise another team's business proposal fairly before giving your own evaluation.","functions":["project outcomes","weigh financial risk","persuade and respond to objections"],"discourse":["presenting a value proposition","handling a challenge","balancing risk and opportunity"],"chunks":["cash-flow pressure","initial investment","break-even point","take a calculated risk","return on investment"],"interactionExpressions":["If that happens, our fallback would be...","The main risk is..., but...","What would make this viable is..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Choose the best first conditional for a realistic business possibility.","options":["If demand increases, we'll add a second delivery shift.","If demand increased, we'll add a second delivery shift.","If demand will increase, we add a second delivery shift."],"answer":"If demand increases, we'll add a second delivery shift.","tag":"grammar:first-conditional"},{"q":"Choose the best second conditional for an unreal or less likely situation.","options":["If we had more capital, we'd open a second branch.","If we have more capital, we'd open a second branch.","If we would have more capital, we opened a second branch."],"answer":"If we had more capital, we'd open a second branch.","tag":"grammar:second-conditional"},{"q":"Which sentence describes a likely consequence rather than an imagined one?","options":["If costs rise again, we'll review our prices.","If costs rose again, we'd review our prices.","If costs had risen, we'd have reviewed our prices."],"answer":"If costs rise again, we'll review our prices.","tag":"grammar:meaning"},{"q":"Which sentence is best for giving hypothetical advice?","options":["If I were you, I'd test the idea before borrowing more money.","If I am you, I'll test the idea.","If I had been you, I test the idea now."],"answer":"If I were you, I'd test the idea before borrowing more money.","tag":"grammar:advice"},{"q":"Complete: “If the pilot attracts enough repeat customers, we ___; if it failed completely, we ___ the concept.”","options":["will expand / would rethink","would expand / will rethink","expanded / rethink"],"answer":"will expand / would rethink","tag":"grammar:contrast"},{"q":"Which version keeps the condition hypothetical?","options":["What would you change if a competitor entered the market?","What will you change if a competitor entered the market?","What would you changed if a competitor enters the market?"],"answer":"What would you change if a competitor entered the market?","tag":"grammar:function"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l9","number":9,"title":"Environment & Climate","outcome":"Interpret environmental information, explain the problem to others, and negotiate a response that balances practical trade-offs.","grammarFocus":"Passive Voice","grammarRule":"Use Passive Voice accurately for community complaint/request email. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Community complaint/request email","vocabulary":["drought","waste","pollution","resource","conservation","shortage"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “drought” to its meaning.","options":["a long period with little or no rain","harmful substances in the environment","the protection of natural resources"],"answer":"a long period with little or no rain","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “waste” to its meaning.","options":["unwanted material","something useful people can use","a situation in which there is not enough of something"],"answer":"unwanted material","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “pollution” to its meaning.","options":["harmful substances in the environment","the protection of natural resources","a long period with little or no rain"],"answer":"harmful substances in the environment","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “resource” to its meaning.","options":["something useful people can use","a situation in which there is not enough of something","unwanted material"],"answer":"something useful people can use","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “conservation” to its meaning.","options":["the protection of natural resources","a long period with little or no rain","harmful substances in the environment"],"answer":"the protection of natural resources","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “shortage” to its meaning.","options":["a situation in which there is not enough of something","unwanted material","something useful people can use"],"answer":"a situation in which there is not enough of something","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “unwanted material”?","options":["waste","resource","shortage"],"answer":"waste","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: the protection of natural resources?","options":["conservation","drought","pollution"],"answer":"conservation","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “a long period with little or no rain”.","answer":"drought","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “Solar power is a form of ___.”","options":["renewable energy","drought","waste"],"answer":"renewable energy","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"In many parts of the Horn of Africa, water is not found as easily as it used to be. Wells are being dug deeper every year, and in some villages, water is now transported by truck from distant sources. New irrigation systems are being introduced to reduce waste, and trees are being planted to help the soil retain moisture. Local communities are increasingly being trained to manage these resources themselves. The situation is complicated because different communities face different constraints. In one area, a new borehole reduced the distance families travelled for water, but the pump later failed because nobody had been trained to maintain it. In another, a solar-powered system worked well technically but could not serve every household during the driest months. Local organisations now argue that infrastructure should not be treated as a one-time delivery. Equipment, training, financing, and rules for fair access all have to be planned together. Some residents also point out that household conservation alone cannot solve a shortage caused by repeated drought and weak public systems. Individual behaviour matters, but larger investment and coordinated resource management are also required.","audioScript":"Reporter: The district has installed three new water points this year. Has that solved the shortage? Engineer: It has reduced travel time for many families, but supply is still limited during the driest weeks. Reporter: What is the main problem now? Engineer: Maintenance and demand. A system can be built quickly, but it needs trained operators, spare parts, and a fair way to manage heavy use. Community leader: We also need better communication. People are more willing to conserve water when they understand how much is available. Reporter: So there isn't one solution? Engineer: Exactly. Infrastructure, local management, and household behaviour have to work together.","questions":[{"q":"Why did one new borehole fail to provide a lasting solution?","options":["The pump failed and nobody had been trained to maintain it","The community had too much water","Solar power was unavailable everywhere"],"answer":"The pump failed and nobody had been trained to maintain it","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"What does the text say infrastructure planning must include?","options":["Equipment, training, financing, and fair-access rules","Equipment only","Household behaviour only"],"answer":"Equipment, training, financing, and fair-access rules","tag":"reading:main-idea"},{"q":"Why can't household conservation solve the whole problem?","options":["Repeated drought and weak public systems also affect supply","Households use no water","Conservation increases drought"],"answer":"Repeated drought and weak public systems also affect supply","tag":"reading:reason"},{"q":"What broader position does the text support?","options":["Individual and institutional responses are both necessary","Only individuals are responsible","Only one technical solution is needed"],"answer":"Individual and institutional responses are both necessary","tag":"reading:stance"},{"q":"What have the new water points improved?","options":["Travel time for many families","The amount of rainfall","Access to imported food"],"answer":"Travel time for many families","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What problem remains during the driest weeks?","options":["Supply is still limited","The water points disappear","Nobody uses water"],"answer":"Supply is still limited","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What does the engineer say a system needs after construction?","options":["Operators, spare parts, and fair management","No maintenance","Only publicity"],"answer":"Operators, spare parts, and fair management","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What combined approach do the speakers support?","options":["Infrastructure, local management, and household behaviour working together","Households solving the problem alone","Building equipment without maintenance planning"],"answer":"Infrastructure, local management, and household behaviour working together","tag":"listening:synthesis"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Dear Sir or Madam,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to community complaint/request email","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to community complaint/request email","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “a situation in which there is not enough of something”?","options":["shortage","waste","resource"],"answer":"shortage","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Kind regards,","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Kind regards,","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write a 100–130 word community email reporting an environmental problem and requesting action.","minWords":100,"maxWords":130,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle passive forms to describe processes and actions without overloading learners with new form.","b2Lift":"Explain cause and effect, interpret evidence, compare policy responses, and distinguish individual action from institutional responsibility.","performance":"Interpret environmental information, explain the problem to others, and negotiate a response that balances practical trade-offs.","pronunciation":"Pause before key evidence and use sentence stress to distinguish cause, consequence, and recommendation.","mediation":"Translate a short set of environmental findings into clear recommendations for a community audience.","functions":["explain an environmental process","interpret evidence","recommend action at different levels"],"discourse":["cause and consequence","moving from evidence to recommendation","qualifying feasibility"],"chunks":["water scarcity","resource management","long-term impact","public infrastructure","adaptation measure"],"interactionExpressions":["The data suggests that...","A realistic response would involve...","That solution may help, but it also..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Which passive sentence keeps the focus on the action rather than the actor?","options":["Three new water points have been installed this year.","The district has installed three new water points this year.","Three new water points installed the district this year."],"answer":"Three new water points have been installed this year.","tag":"grammar:focus"},{"q":"Choose the correct passive for an action happening now.","options":["New storage tanks are being built near the villages.","New storage tanks are built right now by workers every minute.","New storage tanks have being built near the villages."],"answer":"New storage tanks are being built near the villages.","tag":"grammar:continuous-passive"},{"q":"Which sentence best reports a completed investigation?","options":["The system has been reviewed by independent engineers.","The system was reviewing by independent engineers.","The system has reviewed independent engineers."],"answer":"The system has been reviewed by independent engineers.","tag":"grammar:perfect-passive"},{"q":"Choose the best future passive.","options":["Additional training will be provided next month.","Additional training will provide next month.","Additional training is providing next month by itself."],"answer":"Additional training will be provided next month.","tag":"grammar:future-passive"},{"q":"When is the passive especially useful in this lesson?","options":["When the process or result matters more than who performed the action","When the actor must always be emphasised","When no verb is needed"],"answer":"When the process or result matters more than who performed the action","tag":"grammar:meaning"},{"q":"Complete: “Water ___ by truck in some areas, while local operators ___ to manage the new systems.”","options":["is transported / are being trained","transports / train","is transporting / have trained themselves"],"answer":"is transported / are being trained","tag":"grammar:contrast"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l10","number":10,"title":"Relationships & Family","outcome":"Explain a relationship issue from more than one perspective and negotiate a respectful response or boundary.","grammarFocus":"Relative Clauses","grammarRule":"Use Relative Clauses accurately for personal appreciation message. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Personal appreciation message","vocabulary":["relative","supportive","influence","trust","generation","relationship"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “relative” to its meaning.","options":["person in your family","the power to affect a person or situation","people born in a similar period"],"answer":"person in your family","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “supportive” to its meaning.","options":["helpful and encouraging","belief that someone or something is honest and reliable","connection between people"],"answer":"helpful and encouraging","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “influence” to its meaning.","options":["the power to affect a person or situation","people born in a similar period","person in your family"],"answer":"the power to affect a person or situation","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “trust” to its meaning.","options":["belief that someone or something is honest and reliable","connection between people","helpful and encouraging"],"answer":"belief that someone or something is honest and reliable","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “generation” to its meaning.","options":["people born in a similar period","person in your family","the power to affect a person or situation"],"answer":"people born in a similar period","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “relationship” to its meaning.","options":["connection between people","helpful and encouraging","belief that someone or something is honest and reliable"],"answer":"connection between people","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “helpful and encouraging”?","options":["supportive","trust","relationship"],"answer":"supportive","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: people born in a similar period?","options":["generation","relative","influence"],"answer":"generation","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “person in your family”.","answer":"relative","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “My grandmother has always been my ___.”","options":["role model","relative","supportive"],"answer":"role model","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"My grandmother, who raised five children on her own, is the person who taught me the most about resilience. The house where I grew up was always full of relatives who needed a place to stay. My cousin, whose mother passed away when she was young, lived with us for ten years. My grandmother never complained about the responsibility that fell on her shoulders. As the family has grown, the relationships have become more complicated. Younger relatives who study or work in other cities make decisions more independently, while older family members sometimes worry that traditions of mutual support are becoming weaker. The writer has learned that closeness does not mean agreeing on every decision. In one recent disagreement about work, an uncle expected a younger cousin to accept a secure local job, while the cousin wanted to move abroad for further study. Both believed they were protecting the family's future, but they defined security differently. After several difficult conversations, they stopped trying to prove that one side was completely right and began discussing what support the cousin would need if the plan did not work out.","audioScript":"Sister: Mum keeps asking why I don't visit every weekend. I know family matters to her, but I also need time to rest. Brother: Have you told her that directly? Sister: I tried, but she heard it as if I didn't want to see everyone. Brother: Maybe explain the problem rather than just saying no. You could suggest two weekends a month and call on the others. Sister: That sounds more respectful. Brother: And it might help to acknowledge why she values the visits before you explain your boundary. Sister: Yes. I don't want distance from the family. I just need a routine I can actually maintain.","questions":[{"q":"What disagreement does the text use to illustrate generational differences?","options":["Whether a younger cousin should take a local job or study abroad","Where to hold a family dinner","Which sport to play"],"answer":"Whether a younger cousin should take a local job or study abroad","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"What did both sides believe they were protecting?","options":["The family's future","Their personal popularity","A business contract"],"answer":"The family's future","tag":"reading:inference"},{"q":"What changed the conversation?","options":["They stopped trying to prove one side completely right and discussed support needs","They stopped speaking permanently","The cousin accepted the first opinion immediately"],"answer":"They stopped trying to prove one side completely right and discussed support needs","tag":"reading:development"},{"q":"What does the writer suggest closeness does not require?","options":["Agreement on every decision","Mutual support","Communication"],"answer":"Agreement on every decision","tag":"reading:main-idea"},{"q":"Why is the sister uncomfortable with weekly visits?","options":["She needs time to rest","She wants to end the relationship","She is moving abroad tomorrow"],"answer":"She needs time to rest","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"How did the mother interpret the sister's first attempt to say no?","options":["As if she did not want to see the family","As a request for more visits","As a work problem"],"answer":"As if she did not want to see the family","tag":"listening:inference"},{"q":"What practical compromise is suggested?","options":["Visit two weekends a month and call on others","Never visit again","Visit every day"],"answer":"Visit two weekends a month and call on others","tag":"listening:response"},{"q":"What communication strategy does the brother recommend?","options":["Acknowledge the mother's concern and propose a realistic boundary","Stop all family visits","Agree to every request without discussion"],"answer":"Acknowledge the mother's concern and propose a realistic boundary","tag":"listening:pragmatics"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Hi everyone,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Hi everyone,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to personal appreciation message","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to personal appreciation message","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “connection between people”?","options":["relationship","supportive","trust"],"answer":"relationship","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Thanks — I look forward to hearing from you.","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Thanks — I look forward to hearing from you.","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write a 70–100 word appreciation message to someone who has influenced you.","minWords":90,"maxWords":110,"humanGraded":true,"checkpoint":true},"foundation":"Recycle relative clauses to add detail about people and relationships.","b2Lift":"Discuss expectations, boundaries, conflict, generational change, and influence while recognising that different perspectives can all be reasonable.","performance":"Explain a relationship issue from more than one perspective and negotiate a respectful response or boundary.","pronunciation":"Use intonation to signal empathy, reservation, and contrast during sensitive interpersonal discussion.","mediation":"Restate two family members' different viewpoints neutrally and identify possible common ground.","functions":["describe relational influence","express a boundary respectfully","represent another person's perspective"],"discourse":["framing a sensitive issue","contrasting perspectives","finding common ground"],"chunks":["set a boundary","family expectation","support network","generational difference","mutual respect"],"interactionExpressions":["I can see why they might feel that way.","From my perspective...","A compromise could be..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Which sentence adds non-essential information about the grandmother?","options":["My grandmother, who raised five children, taught me resilience.","My grandmother who raised five children taught me resilience.","My grandmother, that raised five children, taught me resilience."],"answer":"My grandmother, who raised five children, taught me resilience.","tag":"grammar:non-defining"},{"q":"Which relative word is best for possession?","options":["whose","who","where"],"answer":"whose","tag":"grammar:relative-pronoun"},{"q":"Choose the best sentence about a place.","options":["The house where I grew up was always full of relatives.","The house which I grew up was always full of relatives.","The house who I grew up was always full of relatives."],"answer":"The house where I grew up was always full of relatives.","tag":"grammar:place"},{"q":"Which sentence identifies one specific cousin from several cousins?","options":["The cousin who moved abroad calls every week.","My cousin, who moved abroad, calls every week.","The cousin, which moved abroad, calls every week."],"answer":"The cousin who moved abroad calls every week.","tag":"grammar:defining"},{"q":"Which version is most natural when the object pronoun can be omitted?","options":["That's the relative I told you about.","That's the relative who I told you about them.","That's the relative which I told you about."],"answer":"That's the relative I told you about.","tag":"grammar:omission"},{"q":"Complete: “My aunt, ___ advice I trust, lives in the town ___ I studied.”","options":["whose / where","who / which","whom / who"],"answer":"whose / where","tag":"grammar:contrast"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l11","number":11,"title":"Media & News","outcome":"Compare two reports about the same event, identify differences in framing, and give an accurate neutral summary to another learner.","grammarFocus":"Reported Speech","grammarRule":"Use Reported Speech accurately for fact-check whatsapp response. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Fact-check WhatsApp response","vocabulary":["source","headline","claim","evidence","reliable","misinformation"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “source” to its meaning.","options":["place information comes from","statement that may need evidence","able to be trusted"],"answer":"place information comes from","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “headline” to its meaning.","options":["title of a news story","facts supporting an idea","false or inaccurate information that is shared"],"answer":"title of a news story","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “claim” to its meaning.","options":["statement that may need evidence","able to be trusted","place information comes from"],"answer":"statement that may need evidence","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “evidence” to its meaning.","options":["facts supporting an idea","false or inaccurate information that is shared","title of a news story"],"answer":"facts supporting an idea","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “reliable” to its meaning.","options":["able to be trusted","place information comes from","statement that may need evidence"],"answer":"able to be trusted","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “misinformation” to its meaning.","options":["false or inaccurate information that is shared","title of a news story","facts supporting an idea"],"answer":"false or inaccurate information that is shared","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “title of a news story”?","options":["headline","evidence","misinformation"],"answer":"headline","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: able to be trusted?","options":["reliable","source","claim"],"answer":"reliable","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “place information comes from”.","answer":"source","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “Always check whether the ___ is credible.”","options":["source","headline","claim"],"answer":"source","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"A journalist said that misinformation spreads six times faster online than accurate news. She explained that many people share headlines without reading the full article. She warned that this habit can shape public opinion in dangerous ways. She also said that readers should always check whether a source is credible before believing or sharing a story. The journalist also explained that misinformation is not always completely invented. A misleading post may contain one accurate fact but remove the context that makes the fact understandable. Headlines can create the same problem when they present the most dramatic interpretation rather than the most representative one. She recommends checking the date, author, original source, and whether other credible outlets report the same event. She also warns readers to notice emotional reactions. If a headline immediately makes someone angry or frightened, that is often the moment to slow down rather than share it. Careful readers do not need to become experts in every topic, but they do need habits that make manipulation more difficult.","audioScript":"Editor: We have two reports about the demonstration. The first says thousands of people filled the city centre, while the second says several hundred attended. Reporter: Do either of them show how they estimated the crowd? Editor: Not clearly. One uses a police statement and the other quotes the organisers. Reporter: So we shouldn't repeat either number as certain. Editor: Exactly. Say that estimates vary and name the sources. Reporter: What about the headline saying the city was brought to a standstill? Editor: That's too strong based on what we know. Two roads were closed for an hour, but public transport continued. Accuracy includes scale, not just individual facts.","questions":[{"q":"Why can a post be misleading even when it contains an accurate fact?","options":["It may remove the context needed to interpret the fact","Accurate facts are always misleading","Dates are never relevant"],"answer":"It may remove the context needed to interpret the fact","tag":"reading:reason"},{"q":"Which checks does the journalist recommend?","options":["Date, author, original source, and other credible reporting","Only the number of likes","Only whether the headline is dramatic"],"answer":"Date, author, original source, and other credible reporting","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"Why should a strong emotional reaction make a reader slow down?","options":["Emotion can make people share before checking","Anger proves a headline is false","Fear always improves accuracy"],"answer":"Emotion can make people share before checking","tag":"reading:inference"},{"q":"What skill does the text mainly promote?","options":["Habits that make manipulation harder","Memorising every news article","Avoiding all media"],"answer":"Habits that make manipulation harder","tag":"reading:main-idea"},{"q":"Why do the two crowd estimates differ?","options":["They come from different sources","Both use the same measurement","No demonstration occurred"],"answer":"They come from different sources","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"How should the reporter present the crowd number?","options":["Say estimates vary and identify the sources","Choose the larger number as fact","Avoid mentioning uncertainty"],"answer":"Say estimates vary and identify the sources","tag":"listening:reporting"},{"q":"What confirmed disruption occurred?","options":["Two roads were closed for an hour","The entire city stopped for a day","All public transport ended"],"answer":"Two roads were closed for an hour","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"Why does the editor reject the phrase “brought to a standstill”?","options":["It exaggerates the scale of the disruption","No roads were closed","The demonstration never happened"],"answer":"It exaggerates the scale of the disruption","tag":"listening:media-literacy"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Hi everyone,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Hi everyone,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to fact-check whatsapp response","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to fact-check whatsapp response","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “false or inaccurate information that is shared”?","options":["misinformation","headline","evidence"],"answer":"misinformation","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Thanks — I look forward to hearing from you.","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Thanks — I look forward to hearing from you.","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write a 70–100 word WhatsApp fact-check response to someone who forwarded questionable news.","minWords":90,"maxWords":110,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle reported speech so learners can accurately relay what another source said.","b2Lift":"Evaluate credibility, distinguish fact from claim, compare framing, qualify certainty, and mediate information without exaggerating it.","performance":"Compare two reports about the same event, identify differences in framing, and give an accurate neutral summary to another learner.","pronunciation":"Use reporting intonation and clear stress to separate the source's claim from your own position.","mediation":"Summarise a report for someone who has not read it while preserving uncertainty and source attribution.","functions":["report a claim accurately","evaluate source credibility","compare media framing"],"discourse":["attributing information","qualifying certainty","contrasting two accounts"],"chunks":["credible source","supporting evidence","misleading headline","verify a claim","editorial bias"],"interactionExpressions":["According to the source...","That claim hasn't been verified yet.","Both reports agree that..., but they differ on..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Choose the best reported version: “I haven't checked the source yet,” she said.","options":["She said she hadn't checked the source yet.","She said she hasn't checked the source yesterday.","She told that she hadn't checked the source yet."],"answer":"She said she hadn't checked the source yet.","tag":"grammar:backshift"},{"q":"Which reporting verb best fits a warning?","options":["She warned readers not to share unverified claims.","She suggested readers that they shared unverified claims.","She admitted readers to not share claims."],"answer":"She warned readers not to share unverified claims.","tag":"grammar:reporting-verb"},{"q":"Choose the best way to report a claim while keeping distance from it.","options":["The organiser claimed that more than 5,000 people attended.","The organiser proved that more than 5,000 people attended.","The organiser knew unquestionably that 5,000 people attended."],"answer":"The organiser claimed that more than 5,000 people attended.","tag":"grammar:stance"},{"q":"Direct: “We'll publish the correction tomorrow.” Reported:","options":["They said they would publish the correction the next day.","They said they will published the correction tomorrow.","They told they would publish the correction yesterday."],"answer":"They said they would publish the correction the next day.","tag":"grammar:time-reference"},{"q":"Which sentence correctly reports a question?","options":["She asked whether I had checked the original source.","She asked had I checked the original source.","She asked whether had I checked the original source."],"answer":"She asked whether I had checked the original source.","tag":"grammar:reported-question"},{"q":"Complete: “The editor ___ that the headline was exaggerated and ___ the reporter to revise it.”","options":["agreed / asked","said / told to","claimed / suggested him"],"answer":"agreed / asked","tag":"grammar:reporting-verbs"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l12","number":12,"title":"Sports & Fitness","outcome":"Analyse a learner or athlete's progress and justify a development plan with evidence, constraints, and measurable targets.","grammarFocus":"Can / Could / Be able to","grammarRule":"Use Can / Could / Be able to accurately for fitness progress update. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Fitness progress update","vocabulary":["stamina","strength","training","ability","discipline","performance"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “stamina” to its meaning.","options":["ability to continue physical effort","the process of learning skills for a job or activity","the habit of following a plan consistently"],"answer":"ability to continue physical effort","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “strength” to its meaning.","options":["something you do well","the skill or power to do something","event with acting, music or dance"],"answer":"something you do well","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “training” to its meaning.","options":["the process of learning skills for a job or activity","the habit of following a plan consistently","ability to continue physical effort"],"answer":"the process of learning skills for a job or activity","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “ability” to its meaning.","options":["the skill or power to do something","event with acting, music or dance","something you do well"],"answer":"the skill or power to do something","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “discipline” to its meaning.","options":["the habit of following a plan consistently","ability to continue physical effort","the process of learning skills for a job or activity"],"answer":"the habit of following a plan consistently","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “performance” to its meaning.","options":["event with acting, music or dance","something you do well","the skill or power to do something"],"answer":"event with acting, music or dance","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “something you do well”?","options":["strength","ability","performance"],"answer":"strength","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: the habit of following a plan consistently?","options":["discipline","stamina","training"],"answer":"discipline","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “ability to continue physical effort”.","answer":"stamina","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “After weeks of training, she achieved a new ___.”","options":["personal best","stamina","strength"],"answer":"personal best","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"Two years ago, Ahmed could barely run for five minutes without stopping. He couldn't afford a gym membership, so he started running on the streets near his home. Slowly, he was able to run longer distances, and last month he was finally able to complete his first marathon. He says he can now run for two hours without feeling exhausted. Ahmed's progress was not simply the result of trying harder every week. After an early knee problem, a coach advised him to reduce distance temporarily and improve strength before increasing his training load. Ahmed found this frustrating because he felt he was moving backwards. However, his running became more consistent once he stopped treating every session as a test. He now tracks sleep, recovery, distance, and how difficult each run feels. The information helps him notice patterns that motivation alone can hide. He has learned that improvement is rarely a straight line: an athlete may become stronger while temporarily running fewer kilometres, or need extra recovery before reaching a new personal best. The ability to adjust a plan is part of performance, not evidence of failure.","audioScript":"Coach: Your endurance has improved, but your pace drops sharply after forty minutes. Runner: I noticed that. I can start comfortably, but I can't maintain the same speed. Coach: The solution isn't to run every session faster. We need one longer easy run, one shorter quality session, and enough recovery between them. Runner: Will I be able to run ten kilometres without stopping in two months? Coach: Probably, if you stay consistent and the knee remains comfortable. I would measure success first by completing the distance at an easy pace, not by chasing a fast time. Runner: That feels more realistic. Coach: Good. A target should guide training, not make every session feel like an exam.","questions":[{"q":"Why did Ahmed temporarily reduce his running distance?","options":["To recover from a knee problem and build strength","Because he lost interest in fitness","Because the coach wanted no training"],"answer":"To recover from a knee problem and build strength","tag":"reading:reason"},{"q":"What changed when he stopped treating every session as a test?","options":["His training became more consistent","His progress stopped completely","He stopped tracking recovery"],"answer":"His training became more consistent","tag":"reading:development"},{"q":"Which information does Ahmed now track?","options":["Sleep, recovery, distance, and perceived difficulty","Only body weight","Only race medals"],"answer":"Sleep, recovery, distance, and perceived difficulty","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"What main principle about progress does the text emphasise?","options":["Improvement is not always a straight line","More effort is always better","Recovery is a sign of failure"],"answer":"Improvement is not always a straight line","tag":"reading:main-idea"},{"q":"What happens to the runner's pace after forty minutes?","options":["It drops sharply","It becomes much faster","It stays exactly the same"],"answer":"It drops sharply","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What does the coach recommend instead of running every session faster?","options":["A mix of easy, quality, and recovery work","Only sprint training","No running for two months"],"answer":"A mix of easy, quality, and recovery work","tag":"listening:response"},{"q":"What condition does the coach attach to the ten-kilometre goal?","options":["Consistency and a comfortable knee","Buying new shoes","Winning a race first"],"answer":"Consistency and a comfortable knee","tag":"listening:condition"},{"q":"How does the coach define the first realistic success target?","options":["Complete the distance comfortably before focusing on speed","Run every session as fast as possible","Ignore the knee problem"],"answer":"Complete the distance comfortably before focusing on speed","tag":"listening:goal-setting"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Hello,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Hello,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to fitness progress update","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to fitness progress update","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “event with acting, music or dance”?","options":["performance","strength","ability"],"answer":"performance","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Thank you.","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Thank you.","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write a 70–100 word fitness progress update describing present ability and a future goal.","minWords":90,"maxWords":110,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle can, could, and be able to to describe familiar changes in ability.","b2Lift":"Evaluate performance, explain limitations and setbacks, connect training choices to evidence, and make realistic development recommendations.","performance":"Analyse a learner or athlete's progress and justify a development plan with evidence, constraints, and measurable targets.","pronunciation":"Use chunking when explaining a training plan and stress measurable goals, limitations, and changes.","mediation":"Turn a coach's detailed feedback into a short, clear plan that another learner can follow.","functions":["describe progress over time","diagnose a limitation","justify a training recommendation"],"discourse":["baseline to progress","problem to intervention","setting measurable targets"],"chunks":["build stamina","training load","recover from injury","personal best","gradual progression"],"interactionExpressions":["Based on your current level...","The main limitation at the moment is...","You'll be more likely to improve if..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Choose the best sentence for a general present ability.","options":["I can run ten kilometres now.","I am able running ten kilometres now.","I could run ten kilometres now after training."],"answer":"I can run ten kilometres now.","tag":"grammar:present-ability"},{"q":"Which sentence refers to a general ability in the past?","options":["I could swim well when I was twelve.","I was able to swim across the river every day when I was twelve.","I can swim well when I was twelve."],"answer":"I could swim well when I was twelve.","tag":"grammar:past-general"},{"q":"Which form is often preferred for one successful achievement in a specific past situation?","options":["She was able to finish the race despite the injury.","She could finish the race despite the injury yesterday.","She can finish the race despite the injury yesterday."],"answer":"She was able to finish the race despite the injury.","tag":"grammar:specific-achievement"},{"q":"Choose the best future form.","options":["With consistent training, you'll be able to run farther.","With consistent training, you'll can run farther.","With consistent training, you could run farther next year definitely."],"answer":"With consistent training, you'll be able to run farther.","tag":"grammar:future-ability"},{"q":"What is the difference between “could” and “managed to” in many past contexts?","options":["“Managed to” highlights success in one difficult situation.","“Could” is only used for the future.","There is never any difference."],"answer":"“Managed to” highlights success in one difficult situation.","tag":"grammar:meaning"},{"q":"Complete: “Two months ago I ___ barely run for ten minutes, but last weekend I ___ complete a full 5K.”","options":["could / was able to","was able to / could","can / could"],"answer":"could / was able to","tag":"grammar:contrast"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l13","number":13,"title":"City vs Countryside","outcome":"Negotiate the best place to live for a specific person or family by weighing competing priorities and defending a final recommendation.","grammarFocus":"Gerunds / Infinitives","grammarRule":"Use Gerunds / Infinitives accurately for facebook advice comment. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Facebook advice comment","vocabulary":["traffic","convenience","community","pace","facility","commute"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “traffic” to its meaning.","options":["vehicles moving on roads","people who live, work, or share interests together","place or service for a purpose"],"answer":"vehicles moving on roads","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “convenience” to its meaning.","options":["the quality of being easy and useful","speed of movement","regular journey between home and work"],"answer":"the quality of being easy and useful","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “community” to its meaning.","options":["people who live, work, or share interests together","place or service for a purpose","vehicles moving on roads"],"answer":"people who live, work, or share interests together","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “pace” to its meaning.","options":["speed of movement","regular journey between home and work","the quality of being easy and useful"],"answer":"speed of movement","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “facility” to its meaning.","options":["place or service for a purpose","vehicles moving on roads","people who live, work, or share interests together"],"answer":"place or service for a purpose","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “commute” to its meaning.","options":["regular journey between home and work","the quality of being easy and useful","speed of movement"],"answer":"regular journey between home and work","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “the quality of being easy and useful”?","options":["convenience","pace","commute"],"answer":"convenience","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: place or service for a purpose?","options":["facility","traffic","community"],"answer":"facility","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “vehicles moving on roads”.","answer":"traffic","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “Heavy traffic ___ makes commuting stressful.”","options":["congestion","traffic","convenience"],"answer":"congestion","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"After finishing university, Faisal enjoyed living in the city for the fast pace and job opportunities, but he found it hard to avoid feeling isolated despite being surrounded by people. He decided to try living in his family's hometown for six months. He loves being close to nature and enjoys knowing his neighbours by name, but he admits he sometimes misses having so many things to do. Faisal's decision was also shaped by factors that are easy to overlook in a simple city-versus-village debate. Housing was cheaper outside the city, but his commute became longer and public transport was less reliable. He gained more space and a stronger relationship with neighbours, yet some specialist services required a full day of travel. His partner, who works remotely, valued the quieter environment, while Faisal sometimes worried that fewer professional events would limit his network. After two years, he no longer describes one place as objectively better. He thinks the right choice depends on life stage, income, family responsibilities, work flexibility, and what a person is willing to exchange for space, convenience, opportunity, or community.","audioScript":"Advisor: Before deciding, let's separate preferences from requirements. Do you need to commute every day? Client: Only twice a week, but my children need reliable schools and my mother needs regular medical appointments. Advisor: That changes the picture. The countryside option is cheaper and more peaceful, but access to specialist healthcare is limited. Client: The city apartment is much smaller, though. Advisor: True. What about the town on the edge of the city? It costs more than the village but less than the centre, and there is a direct bus to the hospital. Client: I hadn't considered that. It may be the best compromise rather than the perfect version of either extreme.","questions":[{"q":"Why is Faisal's decision more complicated than a simple city-versus-village preference?","options":["Work, transport, services, family, and cost create trade-offs","Only house size matters","Both places are identical"],"answer":"Work, transport, services, family, and cost create trade-offs","tag":"reading:main-idea"},{"q":"What benefit did he gain outside the city?","options":["More space and stronger neighbour relationships","A shorter daily commute in every case","More specialist services"],"answer":"More space and stronger neighbour relationships","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"What professional concern does he still have?","options":["Fewer events may limit his network","He has no internet access","He cannot work at all"],"answer":"Fewer events may limit his network","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"What does Faisal conclude about the best place to live?","options":["It depends on priorities and life circumstances","The countryside is always superior","The city is always superior"],"answer":"It depends on priorities and life circumstances","tag":"reading:stance"},{"q":"How often does the client need to commute?","options":["Twice a week","Every day","Never"],"answer":"Twice a week","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"Which family need makes healthcare access important?","options":["The client's mother needs regular appointments","The client wants a gym","The children need a sports stadium"],"answer":"The client's mother needs regular appointments","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"Why is the village option less suitable than it first appears?","options":["Specialist healthcare access is limited","It is more expensive than the city centre","It has no housing"],"answer":"Specialist healthcare access is limited","tag":"listening:inference"},{"q":"Why does the town become an attractive compromise?","options":["It balances cost, space, transport, and healthcare access","It is the cheapest possible option","It removes the need to consider family requirements"],"answer":"It balances cost, space, transport, and healthcare access","tag":"listening:decision-making"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Here is something worth discussing:","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Here is something worth discussing:","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to facebook advice comment","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to facebook advice comment","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “regular journey between home and work”?","options":["commute","convenience","pace"],"answer":"commute","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["What do you think?","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"What do you think?","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write a 90–120 word Facebook comment advising someone choosing between city and countryside.","minWords":90,"maxWords":120,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle common gerund and infinitive patterns while discussing familiar preferences.","b2Lift":"Compare infrastructure, cost, opportunity, community, and lifestyle trade-offs while conceding valid points from the other side.","performance":"Negotiate the best place to live for a specific person or family by weighing competing priorities and defending a final recommendation.","pronunciation":"Use disagreement intonation that sounds firm but collaborative rather than confrontational.","mediation":"Summarise the strongest city and countryside arguments before helping the group identify a compromise.","functions":["compare lifestyle trade-offs","concede an opposing point","negotiate a recommendation"],"discourse":["setting decision criteria","weighing competing advantages","moving toward compromise"],"chunks":["cost of living","public transport network","sense of community","access to services","quality-of-life trade-off"],"interactionExpressions":["That's a fair point, although...","It depends on what we prioritise.","If we weigh everything up, ..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Choose the verb pattern: “I enjoy ___ somewhere with a strong sense of community.”","options":["living","to live","live"],"answer":"living","tag":"grammar:gerund"},{"q":"Choose the verb pattern: “We decided ___ closer to the city.”","options":["to move","moving","move"],"answer":"to move","tag":"grammar:infinitive"},{"q":"Which sentence changes meaning depending on the form after “remember”?","options":["Remember to lock the door / I remember locking the door.","Remember locking the door / I remember to lock the door always mean exactly the same.","Remember lock the door / I remember lock the door."],"answer":"Remember to lock the door / I remember locking the door.","tag":"grammar:meaning-change"},{"q":"Choose the best form after “avoid”.","options":["I avoid driving during rush hour.","I avoid to drive during rush hour.","I avoid drive during rush hour."],"answer":"I avoid driving during rush hour.","tag":"grammar:pattern"},{"q":"Choose the best form after “would like”.","options":["I'd like to live somewhere quieter.","I'd like living somewhere quieter in this context.","I'd like live somewhere quieter."],"answer":"I'd like to live somewhere quieter.","tag":"grammar:pattern"},{"q":"Complete: “I don't mind ___ farther if it means ___ more space.”","options":["commuting / having","to commute / to have","commute / have"],"answer":"commuting / having","tag":"grammar:contrast"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l14","number":14,"title":"Dreams & Ambitions","outcome":"Present a five-year plan, explain assumptions and contingencies, and respond to questions about risks, priorities, and alternative paths.","grammarFocus":"Future Forms","grammarRule":"Use Future Forms accurately for email requesting information. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Email requesting information","vocabulary":["ambition","milestone","opportunity","plan","priority","direction"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “ambition” to its meaning.","options":["strong hope to achieve something","good chance to do something","something more important than other things"],"answer":"strong hope to achieve something","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “milestone” to its meaning.","options":["an important stage showing progress","steps for the future","the course or purpose that guides future action"],"answer":"an important stage showing progress","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “opportunity” to its meaning.","options":["good chance to do something","something more important than other things","strong hope to achieve something"],"answer":"good chance to do something","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “plan” to its meaning.","options":["steps for the future","the course or purpose that guides future action","an important stage showing progress"],"answer":"steps for the future","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “priority” to its meaning.","options":["something more important than other things","strong hope to achieve something","good chance to do something"],"answer":"something more important than other things","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “direction” to its meaning.","options":["the course or purpose that guides future action","an important stage showing progress","steps for the future"],"answer":"the course or purpose that guides future action","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “an important stage showing progress”?","options":["milestone","plan","direction"],"answer":"milestone","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: something more important than other things?","options":["priority","ambition","opportunity"],"answer":"priority","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “strong hope to achieve something”.","answer":"ambition","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “Graduation was a major ___ in her plan.”","options":["milestone","ambition","opportunity"],"answer":"milestone","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"Sagal is going to graduate next year, and she's already planning her next steps. She's going to apply for a master's programme abroad, and she's meeting with an advisor next week to discuss scholarships. She predicts that competition for scholarships will increase, but she's determined to prepare early. In ten years, she believes she'll be running her own consultancy. Sagal has also learned to separate a goal from a timetable. Her ambition to complete a master's degree is strong, but several parts of the plan remain uncertain. Scholarship decisions may affect the country she chooses, family responsibilities could delay her departure, and admission requirements differ between universities. Instead of treating any change as failure, she has created alternative routes. If funding is unavailable next year, she will gain another year of work experience and strengthen her application. She has also set smaller milestones: complete the language requirement, request references, compare programmes, and prepare a realistic budget. The plan gives her direction without pretending that she can control every future event. She sees flexibility as part of commitment rather than the opposite of it.","audioScript":"Mentor: Your goal is clear, but which parts are actually arranged? Sagal: I'm meeting an academic advisor next Tuesday, and I've registered for the language test. The university applications aren't open yet. Mentor: How confident are you about funding? Sagal: I'm hopeful, but I wouldn't say it's guaranteed. There's a good chance I'll qualify for one scholarship, although I need a backup. Mentor: What would you do if funding doesn't come through? Sagal: I would work for another year, save more, and apply again. I don't want one unsuccessful application to end the whole plan. Mentor: Good. A strong plan includes what you'll do when reality changes.","questions":[{"q":"Why does Sagal separate her goal from a fixed timetable?","options":["Several important factors remain uncertain","She has no clear ambition","She refuses to make any plans"],"answer":"Several important factors remain uncertain","tag":"reading:reason"},{"q":"What will she do if funding is unavailable next year?","options":["Gain work experience, save, and apply again","Abandon the goal permanently","Borrow without considering cost"],"answer":"Gain work experience, save, and apply again","tag":"reading:contingency"},{"q":"Which smaller milestones has she identified?","options":["Language requirement, references, programme comparison, and budget","Only buying a plane ticket","Only choosing a city"],"answer":"Language requirement, references, programme comparison, and budget","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"How does Sagal interpret flexibility?","options":["As part of commitment","As proof that the goal is weak","As a reason to avoid milestones"],"answer":"As part of commitment","tag":"reading:stance"},{"q":"Which plans are already arranged?","options":["An advisor meeting and language-test registration","University admission and scholarship approval","A completed master's degree"],"answer":"An advisor meeting and language-test registration","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"How certain is Sagal about scholarship funding?","options":["Hopeful but not certain","Completely guaranteed","Certain she will receive none"],"answer":"Hopeful but not certain","tag":"listening:certainty"},{"q":"What is her fallback plan?","options":["Work another year, save, and apply again","Stop studying permanently","Move without funding"],"answer":"Work another year, save, and apply again","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What makes Sagal's plan resilient?","options":["She has milestones and a fallback if funding is unavailable","She assumes every application will succeed","She refuses to adjust the timetable"],"answer":"She has milestones and a fallback if funding is unavailable","tag":"listening:planning"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Dear Sir or Madam,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to email requesting information","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to email requesting information","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “the course or purpose that guides future action”?","options":["direction","milestone","plan"],"answer":"direction","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Kind regards,","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Kind regards,","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write a 100–130 word email requesting information from a university or training provider.","minWords":100,"maxWords":130,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle will, going to, and Present Continuous for familiar future meanings.","b2Lift":"Express probability, distinguish plans from aspirations, build contingency plans, and discuss milestones and uncertainty realistically.","performance":"Present a five-year plan, explain assumptions and contingencies, and respond to questions about risks, priorities, and alternative paths.","pronunciation":"Use intonation to distinguish confidence, probability, hesitation, and tentative planning.","mediation":"Listen to someone's long-term plan and summarise the key milestones, uncertainties, and fallback option.","functions":["distinguish intention from prediction","express probability","plan for alternative outcomes"],"discourse":["timeline and milestones","certainty and uncertainty","contingency planning"],"chunks":["long-term goal","realistic milestone","fallback option","highly likely","keep my options open"],"interactionExpressions":["There's a good chance that...","If that doesn't work out, ...","By this time next year, I expect to..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Which form is best for a fixed arrangement?","options":["I'm meeting my advisor on Tuesday.","I'll meet my advisor on Tuesday maybe, but nothing is arranged.","I'm going meet my advisor on Tuesday."],"answer":"I'm meeting my advisor on Tuesday.","tag":"grammar:arrangement"},{"q":"Which form best expresses a prior intention?","options":["I'm going to apply for three programmes.","I apply for three programmes next year definitely.","I'm applying for three programmes if I haven't decided yet."],"answer":"I'm going to apply for three programmes.","tag":"grammar:intention"},{"q":"Which sentence is a prediction based mainly on opinion?","options":["I think competition will increase.","Look at those figures; competition is going to increased.","Competition is increasing tomorrow because I think so."],"answer":"I think competition will increase.","tag":"grammar:prediction"},{"q":"Which sentence uses a future form to show probability rather than certainty?","options":["There's a good chance I'll need a backup plan.","I'm needing a backup plan certainly next year.","I will definitely maybe need a backup plan."],"answer":"There's a good chance I'll need a backup plan.","tag":"grammar:certainty"},{"q":"Choose the best sentence for an action expected to be complete before a future point.","options":["By next June, I'll have completed the language requirement.","By next June, I'll complete already the language requirement.","By next June, I'm completing the requirement since a year."],"answer":"By next June, I'll have completed the language requirement.","tag":"grammar:future-perfect"},{"q":"Complete: “I've arranged the interview, so I ___ the adviser on Friday; after that, I think I ___ my final decision.”","options":["am meeting / will make","will meet / am making definitely","meet / am going made"],"answer":"am meeting / will make","tag":"grammar:contrast"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l15","number":15,"title":"Crime & Justice","outcome":"Review an incident, identify contributing factors, and recommend preventative action while distinguishing evidence from assumption.","grammarFocus":"Third Conditional","grammarRule":"Use Third Conditional accurately for incident follow-up email. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Incident follow-up email","vocabulary":["consequence","evidence","incident","prevention","responsibility","justice"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “consequence” to its meaning.","options":["result of an action","an event, especially one requiring attention","duty you manage"],"answer":"result of an action","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “evidence” to its meaning.","options":["facts supporting an idea","action taken to stop a problem before it happens","fair treatment according to law and principle"],"answer":"facts supporting an idea","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “incident” to its meaning.","options":["an event, especially one requiring attention","duty you manage","result of an action"],"answer":"an event, especially one requiring attention","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “prevention” to its meaning.","options":["action taken to stop a problem before it happens","fair treatment according to law and principle","facts supporting an idea"],"answer":"action taken to stop a problem before it happens","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “responsibility” to its meaning.","options":["duty you manage","result of an action","an event, especially one requiring attention"],"answer":"duty you manage","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “justice” to its meaning.","options":["fair treatment according to law and principle","facts supporting an idea","action taken to stop a problem before it happens"],"answer":"fair treatment according to law and principle","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “facts supporting an idea”?","options":["evidence","prevention","justice"],"answer":"evidence","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: duty you manage?","options":["responsibility","consequence","incident"],"answer":"responsibility","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “result of an action”.","answer":"consequence","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “The camera provided important ___.”","options":["evidence","consequence","incident"],"answer":"evidence","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"If the shopkeeper hadn't installed a security camera, the theft would never have been solved. The footage showed exactly what had happened, and police were able to identify the person responsible within a day. If the neighbours hadn't heard the noise and called the police immediately, the thief would have escaped completely. The shopkeeper says that if he had known how useful the camera would be, he would have installed it years earlier. The police report later showed that the incident had several contributing factors. The camera helped identify the person, but poor lighting had made it difficult for witnesses to describe what they saw. The shop's alarm was working, although staff had become used to false alerts and did not react immediately. After the case, nearby businesses met to discuss prevention. Some wanted stronger physical security, while others argued that staff training and better street lighting would reduce risk more effectively. The discussion also raised questions about responsibility. A security system can discourage crime, but it cannot remove every risk, and victims should not be blamed simply because a different precaution might have changed the outcome. The most useful question was therefore not 'Who should have known better?' but 'Which realistic changes are most likely to prevent a similar incident?'","audioScript":"Officer: Let's separate what we know from what we think happened. The camera shows one person entering at 9:14. Witness: I thought there were two people outside. Officer: You may be right, but the second person isn't visible on the recording, so we'll record that as witness information rather than confirmed fact. Shopkeeper: If the streetlight had been working, the witness might have seen more clearly. Officer: That's possible. We should also review why the alarm wasn't acted on immediately. Shopkeeper: Staff had heard several false alarms that week. Officer: Then prevention may require both technical changes and a clearer response procedure. One measure alone probably wouldn't remove the risk completely.","questions":[{"q":"Which factors made the theft more difficult to prevent or investigate?","options":["Poor lighting, alarm fatigue, and security limitations","Too many police officers","A lack of any witnesses or cameras"],"answer":"Poor lighting, alarm fatigue, and security limitations","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"Why had staff not reacted immediately to the alarm?","options":["They had become used to false alerts","The alarm was permanently broken","They were told to ignore all alarms"],"answer":"They had become used to false alerts","tag":"reading:reason"},{"q":"What disagreement emerged after the incident?","options":["Whether physical security or training and lighting should be prioritised","Whether the shop should sell different products","Whether the camera existed"],"answer":"Whether physical security or training and lighting should be prioritised","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"What does the text suggest is the most useful post-incident question?","options":["Which realistic changes are most likely to prevent a similar incident?","Who can be blamed most strongly?","How can every risk be completely removed?"],"answer":"Which realistic changes are most likely to prevent a similar incident?","tag":"reading:main-idea"},{"q":"What does the camera confirm?","options":["One person entered at 9:14","Two people entered at 8:00","Nobody entered the shop"],"answer":"One person entered at 9:14","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"How is the possible second person classified?","options":["Witness information rather than confirmed fact","Confirmed camera evidence","Irrelevant information that must be deleted"],"answer":"Witness information rather than confirmed fact","tag":"listening:evidence"},{"q":"What issue may have reduced staff response to the alarm?","options":["Repeated false alarms","A missing security camera","A power cut lasting a week"],"answer":"Repeated false alarms","tag":"listening:reason"},{"q":"Why does the officer separate witness information from confirmed fact?","options":["To avoid presenting an unverified detail as certain","Because witness statements are never useful","Because the camera recorded two people clearly"],"answer":"To avoid presenting an unverified detail as certain","tag":"listening:evidence"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Dear Sir or Madam,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to incident follow-up email","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to incident follow-up email","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “fair treatment according to law and principle”?","options":["justice","evidence","prevention"],"answer":"justice","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Kind regards,","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Kind regards,","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write a 100–130 word follow-up email explaining a non-violent incident and what could have prevented it.","minWords":110,"maxWords":130,"humanGraded":true,"checkpoint":true},"foundation":"Recycle the third conditional to reflect on past actions and alternative outcomes.","b2Lift":"Analyse responsibility, prevention, evidence, unintended consequences, and fairness rather than using hypothetical grammar only for personal regret.","performance":"Review an incident, identify contributing factors, and recommend preventative action while distinguishing evidence from assumption.","pronunciation":"Stress the condition and consequence clearly when explaining alternative outcomes and responsibility.","mediation":"Summarise witness information neutrally and distinguish confirmed facts from interpretation.","functions":["analyse an incident","evaluate responsibility","recommend prevention"],"discourse":["fact versus assumption","causal chain","counterfactual evaluation"],"chunks":["supporting evidence","preventative measure","contributing factor","reasonable precaution","unintended consequence"],"interactionExpressions":["Based on the evidence we have...","If that measure had been in place...","We should be careful not to assume that..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Choose the correct third conditional.","options":["If the camera hadn't been installed, the theft might not have been solved.","If the camera wasn't installed, the theft might not have been solved yesterday.","If the camera wouldn't have been installed, the theft hadn't been solved."],"answer":"If the camera hadn't been installed, the theft might not have been solved.","tag":"grammar:third-conditional"},{"q":"Which sentence expresses a possible alternative past result, not a certain one?","options":["If the streetlight had worked, the witness might have seen more clearly.","If the streetlight had worked, the witness will have seen clearly.","If the streetlight worked, the witness had seen clearly."],"answer":"If the streetlight had worked, the witness might have seen more clearly.","tag":"grammar:modal-result"},{"q":"Choose the best sentence for past regret.","options":["If we had reviewed the alarm procedure earlier, we could have responded faster.","If we reviewed the alarm procedure earlier, we could respond yesterday.","If we would review the alarm procedure, we had responded faster."],"answer":"If we had reviewed the alarm procedure earlier, we could have responded faster.","tag":"grammar:counterfactual"},{"q":"Which question correctly asks about an unreal past alternative?","options":["What would you have done if you had been responsible for the shop?","What did you have done if you would be responsible?","What would you do if you had been responsible yesterday?"],"answer":"What would you have done if you had been responsible for the shop?","tag":"grammar:function"},{"q":"Complete: “If staff ___ so many false alarms, they ___ the warning more seriously.”","options":["hadn't heard / might have taken","didn't hear / might have took","wouldn't hear / had taken"],"answer":"hadn't heard / might have taken","tag":"grammar:contrast"},{"q":"Which sentence avoids unfair certainty when discussing an alternative past?","options":["Better lighting might have helped witnesses see more clearly.","Better lighting would definitely have prevented all crime.","Better lighting had prevented the incident if installed."],"answer":"Better lighting might have helped witnesses see more clearly.","tag":"grammar:pragmatics"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l16","number":16,"title":"Science & Innovation","outcome":"Evaluate several explanations for an unusual claim and defend the most plausible conclusion while acknowledging uncertainty.","grammarFocus":"Modals of Deduction","grammarRule":"Use Modals of Deduction accurately for online response to an unusual claim. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Online response to an unusual claim","vocabulary":["evidence","theory","discovery","experiment","signal","innovation"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “evidence” to its meaning.","options":["facts supporting an idea","something learned or found for the first time","a sign that communicates information"],"answer":"facts supporting an idea","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “theory” to its meaning.","options":["an explanation based on ideas and evidence","test used to learn something","new idea, method or product"],"answer":"an explanation based on ideas and evidence","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “discovery” to its meaning.","options":["something learned or found for the first time","a sign that communicates information","facts supporting an idea"],"answer":"something learned or found for the first time","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “experiment” to its meaning.","options":["test used to learn something","new idea, method or product","an explanation based on ideas and evidence"],"answer":"test used to learn something","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “signal” to its meaning.","options":["a sign that communicates information","facts supporting an idea","something learned or found for the first time"],"answer":"a sign that communicates information","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “innovation” to its meaning.","options":["new idea, method or product","an explanation based on ideas and evidence","test used to learn something"],"answer":"new idea, method or product","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “an explanation based on ideas and evidence”?","options":["theory","experiment","innovation"],"answer":"theory","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: a sign that communicates information?","options":["signal","evidence","discovery"],"answer":"signal","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “facts supporting an idea”.","answer":"evidence","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “The experiment led to a major ___.”","options":["breakthrough","evidence","theory"],"answer":"breakthrough","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"Scientists recently detected a strange signal from deep space. Some researchers believe it might be caused by a distant star, while others think it could be interference from equipment on Earth. A few scientists have joked that it must be something more exciting, though most agree that it can't be evidence of alien life — at least not yet. Further research may reveal the answer within the next few years. The team then compared the signal with earlier observations and found that a similar pattern had appeared twice before, although it was much weaker. That made a simple equipment failure less likely, but it did not prove an extraordinary explanation. One group checked whether satellites or aircraft could have caused interference, while another examined natural sources such as pulsars and magnetic activity. As each possibility was tested, researchers updated their confidence rather than defending their first guess. The lead scientist warned journalists that an unexplained signal is simply unexplained; it is not evidence for whichever dramatic theory attracts the most attention. The most responsible scientific conclusion may remain provisional for a long time. Uncertainty, in this context, is not ignorance but an accurate description of what the evidence can and cannot support.","audioScript":"Researcher 1: The signal appears at the same frequency in three separate recordings, so it can't just be random noise. Researcher 2: Agreed, but it could still be local interference. Have we compared the timing with satellite passes? Researcher 1: Yes. None of the known satellites were in the right position. Researcher 3: What about the distant-star theory? Researcher 2: It's plausible, but we don't yet have a second instrument confirming the signal. Researcher 1: Then our conclusion should stay cautious. We can rule out some simple explanations, but we can't claim we know the source. Researcher 3: That's less exciting for the headline, but much more accurate scientifically. This example also prepares learners to compare competing explanations, evaluate evidence quality, challenge assumptions, and revise conclusions when new information appears. The important point is not to use difficult language for its own sake, but to understand the situation, choose language that fits the purpose, and communicate the reasoning clearly to another person.","questions":[{"q":"Why did repeated recordings make simple equipment failure less likely?","options":["A similar signal had appeared on separate occasions","The equipment had never been tested","The signal appeared only once"],"answer":"A similar signal had appeared on separate occasions","tag":"reading:inference"},{"q":"Which possible causes did researchers investigate?","options":["Satellites, aircraft, and natural astronomical sources","Only weather on Earth","Only human speech"],"answer":"Satellites, aircraft, and natural astronomical sources","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"How did the researchers treat their first explanations?","options":["They updated confidence as evidence changed","They defended the first theory regardless of evidence","They published a final answer immediately"],"answer":"They updated confidence as evidence changed","tag":"reading:scientific-reasoning"},{"q":"What is the lead scientist's main warning?","options":["Unexplained does not mean a dramatic theory has been proven","Uncertainty should never be reported","The most exciting explanation is usually correct"],"answer":"Unexplained does not mean a dramatic theory has been proven","tag":"reading:stance"},{"q":"What makes random noise unlikely?","options":["The same frequency appears in three recordings","The researchers dislike that explanation","The signal was very loud"],"answer":"The same frequency appears in three recordings","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What possible explanation remains after checking satellites?","options":["A distant-star source","A known satellite in the correct position","A confirmed equipment failure"],"answer":"A distant-star source","tag":"listening:inference"},{"q":"What evidence is still missing?","options":["Confirmation from a second instrument","Any recording of the signal","Any researcher opinion"],"answer":"Confirmation from a second instrument","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"Why do the researchers keep their conclusion cautious?","options":["They lack independent confirmation even though some explanations are less likely","They have already proved the source","They believe uncertainty should never be reported"],"answer":"They lack independent confirmation even though some explanations are less likely","tag":"listening:scientific-reasoning"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Hello,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Hello,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to online response to an unusual claim","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to online response to an unusual claim","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “new idea, method or product”?","options":["innovation","theory","experiment"],"answer":"innovation","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Thank you.","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Thank you.","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write an 80–110 word online response to an unusual claim, ranking possible explanations by certainty.","minWords":110,"maxWords":130,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle modals of deduction and speculation to express different levels of certainty.","b2Lift":"Compare competing explanations, evaluate evidence quality, challenge assumptions, and revise conclusions when new information appears.","performance":"Evaluate several explanations for an unusual claim and defend the most plausible conclusion while acknowledging uncertainty.","pronunciation":"Use intonation to distinguish strong deduction, tentative possibility, and open uncertainty.","mediation":"Explain the evidence and competing theories to someone who has not seen the original information.","functions":["express degrees of certainty","evaluate competing explanations","revise a conclusion"],"discourse":["claim to evidence","ranking explanations","qualifying a conclusion"],"chunks":["plausible explanation","supporting data","working theory","rule out a possibility","remain uncertain"],"interactionExpressions":["The most plausible explanation is...","That could account for..., but...","We don't have enough evidence to rule out..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"The same signal appears in three independent recordings. Which deduction is strongest?","options":["It can't simply be random noise.","It might definitely be random noise.","It must be an alien message."],"answer":"It can't simply be random noise.","tag":"grammar:deduction"},{"q":"There is some evidence for a distant-star explanation, but not enough to be certain. Choose the best modal.","options":["It could be caused by a distant star.","It must be caused by a distant star.","It can't be caused by a distant star."],"answer":"It could be caused by a distant star.","tag":"grammar:possibility"},{"q":"Researchers checked the satellite records and found no match. Which sentence is best?","options":["It can't have been caused by any of the known satellites they checked.","It must have been caused by a known satellite.","It might have definitely been a known satellite."],"answer":"It can't have been caused by any of the known satellites they checked.","tag":"grammar:past-deduction"},{"q":"The equipment was functioning normally, but a fault is still possible. Choose the most cautious form.","options":["There might have been an intermittent equipment fault.","There must have been an equipment fault.","There can't have been any equipment fault."],"answer":"There might have been an intermittent equipment fault.","tag":"grammar:certainty"},{"q":"Which phrase best signals a strong conclusion based on evidence rather than personal belief?","options":["The data strongly suggests that...","I just feel that...","There is no doubt because I prefer this idea."],"answer":"The data strongly suggests that...","tag":"grammar:stance"},{"q":"Complete: “The signal ___ be local interference, but it ___ have come from the known satellite because the timing doesn't match.”","options":["might / can't","must / might","can't / must"],"answer":"might / can't","tag":"grammar:contrast"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l17","number":17,"title":"Arts & Entertainment","outcome":"Give a developed review or discussion of a creative work or creative life decision and respond thoughtfully to a different interpretation.","grammarFocus":"Mixed Conditionals","grammarRule":"Use Mixed Conditionals accurately for review/comment about a creative experience. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Review/comment about a creative experience","vocabulary":["creative","performance","audition","talent","inspiration","audience"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “creative” to its meaning.","options":["involving imagination","a performance used to select someone for a role","an idea or person that encourages creative action"],"answer":"involving imagination","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “performance” to its meaning.","options":["event with acting, music or dance","a natural or developed ability to do something well","people watching or listening"],"answer":"event with acting, music or dance","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “audition” to its meaning.","options":["a performance used to select someone for a role","an idea or person that encourages creative action","involving imagination"],"answer":"a performance used to select someone for a role","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “talent” to its meaning.","options":["a natural or developed ability to do something well","people watching or listening","event with acting, music or dance"],"answer":"a natural or developed ability to do something well","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “inspiration” to its meaning.","options":["an idea or person that encourages creative action","involving imagination","a performance used to select someone for a role"],"answer":"an idea or person that encourages creative action","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “audience” to its meaning.","options":["people watching or listening","event with acting, music or dance","a natural or developed ability to do something well"],"answer":"people watching or listening","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “event with acting, music or dance”?","options":["performance","talent","audience"],"answer":"performance","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: an idea or person that encourages creative action?","options":["inspiration","creative","audition"],"answer":"inspiration","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “involving imagination”.","answer":"creative","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “The audience gave the ___ a standing ovation.”","options":["performance","creative","audition"],"answer":"performance","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"If Bashir hadn't kept practising during difficult years, he wouldn't be a professional musician today. He almost gave up when his family couldn't afford lessons, but a local teacher offered to train him for free. If that teacher hadn't believed in him, Bashir probably wouldn't be performing on stages across the country now. He says that if he gave up creativity today, he wouldn't feel like himself. Bashir's later success also changed the way he thinks about talent. When he was younger, he assumed professional musicians were people who had been naturally confident from the beginning. His own experience was much less simple. He had periods when he practised consistently and others when financial pressure left almost no time for music. The teacher who supported him did not promise that persistence would guarantee fame. Instead, she helped him develop specific habits: listening critically, recording rehearsals, performing for small audiences, and asking for detailed feedback. Bashir now says that if he had measured success only by public recognition, he might have stopped again. The smaller evidence of improvement—cleaner timing, stronger interpretation, better audience connection—kept the long-term goal meaningful before any major opportunity appeared.","audioScript":"Reviewer 1: I thought the film was visually impressive, but the story felt too predictable. Reviewer 2: That's interesting. I saw the simple story as deliberate because it kept attention on the characters' relationship. Reviewer 1: I can see that, although some scenes repeated the same emotional point. Reviewer 2: True. The middle section could have been shorter. But the final scene changed how I understood several earlier choices. Reviewer 1: I agree about the ending. If the director hadn't included that scene, I would have judged the whole film more negatively. Reviewer 2: So we differ on the pacing, but we both think the ending gives the film most of its impact.","questions":[{"q":"What assumption about talent did Bashir once hold?","options":["Professionals had been naturally confident from the beginning","Talent never requires practice","Only wealthy people can become musicians"],"answer":"Professionals had been naturally confident from the beginning","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"What did his teacher help him develop?","options":["Specific habits for practice, feedback, and performance","A guarantee of fame","A way to avoid audiences"],"answer":"Specific habits for practice, feedback, and performance","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"Why might Bashir have stopped if he measured success only by recognition?","options":["Smaller evidence of improvement would have seemed meaningless","Recognition came immediately","He disliked music"],"answer":"Smaller evidence of improvement would have seemed meaningless","tag":"reading:inference"},{"q":"What does the text suggest sustained his long-term goal?","options":["Visible small improvements before major opportunities appeared","Guaranteed future fame","Avoiding feedback"],"answer":"Visible small improvements before major opportunities appeared","tag":"reading:main-idea"},{"q":"What is Reviewer 1's main criticism?","options":["The story feels too predictable","The film has no visual quality","The ending is missing"],"answer":"The story feels too predictable","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"How does Reviewer 2 interpret the simple story?","options":["As a deliberate way to focus on the characters","As accidental poor writing","As evidence the film should be shorter than ten minutes"],"answer":"As a deliberate way to focus on the characters","tag":"listening:interpretation"},{"q":"What criticism does Reviewer 2 accept?","options":["The middle section could be shorter","The final scene should be removed","The acting is impossible to understand"],"answer":"The middle section could be shorter","tag":"listening:concession"},{"q":"What do the two reviewers ultimately agree about?","options":["The ending gives the film much of its impact","The film has no weaknesses","The story is completely unpredictable"],"answer":"The ending gives the film much of its impact","tag":"listening:evaluation"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Here is something worth discussing:","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Here is something worth discussing:","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to review/comment about a creative experience","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to review/comment about a creative experience","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “people watching or listening”?","options":["audience","performance","talent"],"answer":"audience","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["What do you think?","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"What do you think?","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write a 90–120 word review/comment about a film, book, performance or creative experience.","minWords":110,"maxWords":130,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle mixed conditionals to connect past choices with present consequences.","b2Lift":"Evaluate creative work, interpret artistic choices, support opinions with evidence, and distinguish personal taste from broader evaluation.","performance":"Give a developed review or discussion of a creative work or creative life decision and respond thoughtfully to a different interpretation.","pronunciation":"Use expressive but controlled prominence to communicate evaluation without sounding exaggerated.","mediation":"Explain another person's interpretation of a creative work before comparing it with your own.","functions":["interpret creative choices","support an evaluation","compare different responses"],"discourse":["description to interpretation","evidence for judgement","personal taste versus evaluation"],"chunks":["creative direction","emotional impact","artistic choice","critical response","develop a distinctive style"],"interactionExpressions":["What stood out to me was...","I can see that interpretation, although...","My judgement is based mainly on..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Choose the mixed conditional with a past condition and present result.","options":["If Bashir hadn't continued practising, he wouldn't be a professional musician now.","If Bashir didn't continue practising, he wouldn't have been a musician now.","If Bashir hadn't continued practising, he won't be a musician now."],"answer":"If Bashir hadn't continued practising, he wouldn't be a professional musician now.","tag":"grammar:past-present"},{"q":"Choose the mixed conditional with a present condition and past result.","options":["If I were more confident, I would have auditioned last year.","If I had been more confident, I would audition last year.","If I am more confident, I would have auditioned last year."],"answer":"If I were more confident, I would have auditioned last year.","tag":"grammar:present-past"},{"q":"Which sentence connects a past choice to a current skill level?","options":["If she hadn't stopped practising, she might be a stronger pianist now.","If she didn't stop practising, she might have been a stronger pianist now.","If she won't stop practising, she is stronger now."],"answer":"If she hadn't stopped practising, she might be a stronger pianist now.","tag":"grammar:meaning"},{"q":"Choose the best sentence for a present personality trait affecting a past opportunity.","options":["If he weren't so cautious, he would have accepted the role last year.","If he hadn't been so cautious now, he accepts the role last year.","If he isn't so cautious, he would accept the role yesterday."],"answer":"If he weren't so cautious, he would have accepted the role last year.","tag":"grammar:meaning"},{"q":"Complete: “If the director ___ that final scene, I ___ the film much more negatively now.”","options":["hadn't included / would view","didn't include / would have viewed","wouldn't include / viewed"],"answer":"hadn't included / would view","tag":"grammar:contrast"},{"q":"Which question invites reflection on how a past choice affects the present?","options":["How would your life be different now if you had made another choice?","How would your life have been now if you make another choice?","How is your life different now if you would make another choice?"],"answer":"How would your life be different now if you had made another choice?","tag":"grammar:function"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l18","number":18,"title":"Global Issues","outcome":"Use two short sources to brief a group on a global issue, compare viewpoints, and defend a practical response while acknowledging trade-offs.","grammarFocus":"Passive Reporting","grammarRule":"Use Passive Reporting accurately for social awareness post. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Social awareness post","vocabulary":["inequality","access","cooperation","policy","development","challenge"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “inequality” to its meaning.","options":["unfair difference between groups","working together toward a shared result","the process of growing or improving"],"answer":"unfair difference between groups","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “access” to its meaning.","options":["ability or right to use something","an official rule or plan used by an organisation","something difficult"],"answer":"ability or right to use something","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “cooperation” to its meaning.","options":["working together toward a shared result","the process of growing or improving","unfair difference between groups"],"answer":"working together toward a shared result","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “policy” to its meaning.","options":["an official rule or plan used by an organisation","something difficult","ability or right to use something"],"answer":"an official rule or plan used by an organisation","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “development” to its meaning.","options":["the process of growing or improving","unfair difference between groups","working together toward a shared result"],"answer":"the process of growing or improving","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “challenge” to its meaning.","options":["something difficult","ability or right to use something","an official rule or plan used by an organisation"],"answer":"something difficult","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “ability or right to use something”?","options":["access","policy","challenge"],"answer":"access","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: the process of growing or improving?","options":["development","inequality","cooperation"],"answer":"development","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “unfair difference between groups”.","answer":"inequality","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “The campaign raised public ___ of the issue.”","options":["awareness","inequality","access"],"answer":"awareness","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"It is often said that the world has never been more connected, yet it is also believed by many economists that inequality between regions continues to grow. It is reported that access to clean water has improved significantly over the past twenty years, but it is estimated that hundreds of millions of people still lack basic sanitation. It is thought that solving these problems will require cooperation between governments, businesses, and individuals. These improvements, however, are distributed unevenly. A country may record economic growth while large groups continue to lack reliable healthcare, education, or secure employment. Humanitarian assistance can protect people during an emergency, but emergency support alone rarely changes the structures that created vulnerability. Development programmes face their own difficulties: projects designed internationally may overlook local priorities, while locally led programmes can struggle to obtain stable funding. Researchers therefore increasingly argue that effective responses need both immediate protection and long-term institutional change. There is also disagreement about how success should be measured. National averages can improve while particular regions fall further behind. For that reason, many analysts recommend combining economic indicators with information about access, participation, security, and people's own assessment of whether their lives are improving.","audioScript":"Presenter: Source A argues that international aid should focus more strongly on immediate humanitarian needs because people cannot benefit from long-term reforms if they are not safe now. Analyst: Source B doesn't reject emergency aid, but it warns that repeated short-term funding can leave the underlying system unchanged. Presenter: So are the sources in conflict? Analyst: Partly, but not completely. Both accept that urgent needs matter. The disagreement is about balance and timing. Presenter: What would a combined approach look like? Analyst: Protect people during the crisis, but design the emergency response so that local institutions gain capacity rather than becoming permanently dependent on external delivery. Presenter: That sounds more difficult than choosing one model. Analyst: It is, but complex problems rarely have one-layer solutions.","questions":[{"q":"Why can national economic growth hide continuing inequality?","options":["Average improvement may not reach all groups or regions","Economic growth always reduces access","National statistics never contain useful information"],"answer":"Average improvement may not reach all groups or regions","tag":"reading:inference"},{"q":"What limitation of emergency humanitarian support does the text identify?","options":["It may protect people now without changing underlying vulnerability","It always damages local institutions","It cannot respond to urgent needs"],"answer":"It may protect people now without changing underlying vulnerability","tag":"reading:reason"},{"q":"What problem can internationally designed programmes face?","options":["They may overlook local priorities","They always receive too little data","They cannot include funding"],"answer":"They may overlook local priorities","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"Why do analysts recommend several kinds of success indicators?","options":["Averages alone may hide unequal access and lived experience","Economic data should never be used","Only personal opinions matter"],"answer":"Averages alone may hide unequal access and lived experience","tag":"reading:main-idea"},{"q":"What does Source A prioritise?","options":["Immediate humanitarian needs","Ending all emergency funding","Long-term reform only"],"answer":"Immediate humanitarian needs","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What concern does Source B raise?","options":["Repeated short-term funding can leave the underlying system unchanged","Emergency aid is never needed","Local institutions should be removed"],"answer":"Repeated short-term funding can leave the underlying system unchanged","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"Where do the sources actually agree?","options":["Urgent needs matter","Long-term institutions are irrelevant","Only international organisations should act"],"answer":"Urgent needs matter","tag":"listening:synthesis"},{"q":"How does the analyst reconcile the two sources?","options":["Combine immediate protection with long-term local capacity building","Choose emergency aid and ignore institutions","End all external assistance"],"answer":"Combine immediate protection with long-term local capacity building","tag":"listening:synthesis"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Here is something worth discussing:","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Here is something worth discussing:","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to social awareness post","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to social awareness post","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “something difficult”?","options":["challenge","access","policy"],"answer":"challenge","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["What do you think?","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"What do you think?","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write a 100–130 word social-media awareness post explaining a global issue and one realistic action.","minWords":110,"maxWords":130,"humanGraded":true,"checkpoint":true},"foundation":"Recycle passive reporting structures to report widely held claims and public positions.","b2Lift":"Synthesise sources, compare viewpoints, distinguish evidence from general belief, and evaluate solutions to complex global problems.","performance":"Use two short sources to brief a group on a global issue, compare viewpoints, and defend a practical response while acknowledging trade-offs.","pronunciation":"Use formal presentation rhythm, clear pausing, and prominence on claims, evidence, and contrast.","mediation":"Combine key information from two sources into a neutral summary before giving your own position.","functions":["synthesise source information","compare public viewpoints","evaluate a proposed solution"],"discourse":["source synthesis","claim versus evidence","problem-solution evaluation"],"chunks":["structural inequality","humanitarian response","long-term intervention","international cooperation","unintended impact"],"interactionExpressions":["Both sources highlight..., although...","The evidence is strongest when...","A workable response would need to..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Choose the best impersonal reporting structure.","options":["It is widely believed that access to education improves opportunity.","It widely believes that access to education improves opportunity.","It is believing that education improves opportunity."],"answer":"It is widely believed that access to education improves opportunity.","tag":"grammar:impersonal"},{"q":"Choose the personal passive reporting structure.","options":["The programme is reported to have reached 20,000 learners.","The programme reports to have reached 20,000 learners by itself.","The programme is reported that it reached to 20,000 learners."],"answer":"The programme is reported to have reached 20,000 learners.","tag":"grammar:personal"},{"q":"The original claim refers to a past event. Choose the best form.","options":["The minister is said to have approved the plan last month.","The minister is said to approve the plan last month.","The minister said to have approve the plan last month."],"answer":"The minister is said to have approved the plan last month.","tag":"grammar:perfect-infinitive"},{"q":"Which sentence keeps appropriate distance from an uncertain public claim?","options":["It has been suggested that the policy may increase access.","The policy definitely increases access because people say so.","It is proved that the policy may definitely work."],"answer":"It has been suggested that the policy may increase access.","tag":"grammar:stance"},{"q":"Choose the best way to report a current ongoing trend.","options":["Inequality is believed to be increasing in some regions.","Inequality is believed increasing in some regions.","Inequality believes to increase in some regions."],"answer":"Inequality is believed to be increasing in some regions.","tag":"grammar:continuous-infinitive"},{"q":"Complete: “The programme ___ to be effective, but its long-term impact ___ to have been measured fully yet.”","options":["is thought / is not believed","thinks / doesn't believe","is thinking / isn't believing"],"answer":"is thought / is not believed","tag":"grammar:contrast"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l19","number":19,"title":"Free Time & Hobbies","outcome":"Recommend a meaningful leisure activity for a specific person's needs and justify the recommendation against realistic constraints.","grammarFocus":"Phrasal Verbs","grammarRule":"Use Phrasal Verbs accurately for whatsapp hobby recommendation. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"WhatsApp hobby recommendation","vocabulary":["leisure","relaxation","creativity","hobby","routine","interest"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “leisure” to its meaning.","options":["free time outside work","the ability to produce original ideas","regular way of doing something"],"answer":"free time outside work","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “relaxation” to its meaning.","options":["time or activity that reduces tension","free-time activity","something you want to know more about"],"answer":"time or activity that reduces tension","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “creativity” to its meaning.","options":["the ability to produce original ideas","regular way of doing something","free time outside work"],"answer":"the ability to produce original ideas","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “hobby” to its meaning.","options":["free-time activity","something you want to know more about","time or activity that reduces tension"],"answer":"free-time activity","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “routine” to its meaning.","options":["regular way of doing something","free time outside work","the ability to produce original ideas"],"answer":"regular way of doing something","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “interest” to its meaning.","options":["something you want to know more about","time or activity that reduces tension","free-time activity"],"answer":"something you want to know more about","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “time or activity that reduces tension”?","options":["relaxation","hobby","interest"],"answer":"relaxation","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: regular way of doing something?","options":["routine","leisure","creativity"],"answer":"routine","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “free time outside work”.","answer":"leisure","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “Reading helps me ___ after work.”","options":["unwind","leisure","relaxation"],"answer":"unwind","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"Layla recently took up gardening after giving up her old hobby of painting, which she found too stressful to keep up with alongside work. She now spends weekends hanging out in her small garden, growing vegetables and flowers. She says it helps her switch off completely, and she has even talked her sister into trying it out too. Her sister has since got into growing herbs on her balcony. Gardening also changed the way Layla thinks about free time. She had previously treated hobbies as optional activities that she could do only after every responsibility was complete, which meant they were usually postponed indefinitely. Gardening required a different rhythm because plants could not always wait for a convenient weekend. Surprisingly, the small commitment helped her protect time rather than lose it. She now spends fewer evenings scrolling online because she has something practical she wants to check, improve, or learn. The hobby has also widened her social circle: neighbours exchange seeds, compare techniques, and occasionally solve problems together. Layla does not think everyone should take up gardening. Her broader lesson is that a useful hobby should fit a person's energy, budget, available space, and reason for wanting leisure in the first place.","audioScript":"Friend: I need something to do after work that gets me away from a screen, but I don't want an expensive hobby. Layla: Have you thought about walking photography? Friend: Isn't photography expensive? Layla: It can be, but you can start with your phone and focus on composition rather than equipment. Friend: I also want something social. Layla: Then a weekend walking group might suit you better. You could still take photos, but the main activity would be exploring places with other people. Friend: That sounds more realistic than buying lots of equipment. Layla: Exactly. The best hobby isn't the most impressive one. It's the one you're likely to keep doing.","questions":[{"q":"Why had Layla often postponed hobbies in the past?","options":["She treated them as optional until every responsibility was finished","She had no interests","Her work required gardening"],"answer":"She treated them as optional until every responsibility was finished","tag":"reading:reason"},{"q":"How did gardening unexpectedly help her manage time?","options":["The small regular commitment helped her protect leisure time","It removed all responsibilities","It required no routine"],"answer":"The small regular commitment helped her protect leisure time","tag":"reading:inference"},{"q":"What social benefit did gardening create?","options":["Connections with neighbours through sharing and problem-solving","Less contact with other people","A reason to avoid the community"],"answer":"Connections with neighbours through sharing and problem-solving","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"What does Layla think should determine whether a hobby suits someone?","options":["Energy, budget, space, and personal purpose","How impressive it looks online","Whether everyone else does it"],"answer":"Energy, budget, space, and personal purpose","tag":"reading:main-idea"},{"q":"What does the friend want from a hobby?","options":["Less screen time, low cost, and some social contact","Expensive equipment","A completely solitary indoor activity"],"answer":"Less screen time, low cost, and some social contact","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"Why does Layla initially suggest phone photography?","options":["It can begin without expensive equipment","It requires professional cameras","It guarantees a new career"],"answer":"It can begin without expensive equipment","tag":"listening:reason"},{"q":"Why does she then prefer a walking group?","options":["It better matches the friend's social goal","It costs more","It avoids being outdoors"],"answer":"It better matches the friend's social goal","tag":"listening:adaptation"},{"q":"Why does Layla recommend a walking group rather than expensive photography equipment?","options":["It better matches the friend's budget and desire for social activity","The friend dislikes being outdoors","Photography cannot be learned with a phone"],"answer":"It better matches the friend's budget and desire for social activity","tag":"listening:recommendation"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Hi everyone,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Hi everyone,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to whatsapp hobby recommendation","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to whatsapp hobby recommendation","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “something you want to know more about”?","options":["interest","relaxation","hobby"],"answer":"interest","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Thanks — I look forward to hearing from you.","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Thanks — I look forward to hearing from you.","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write a 70–90 word WhatsApp message convincing a friend to try a hobby.","minWords":110,"maxWords":130,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle common phrasal verbs so learners can talk fluently about starting, continuing, and stopping hobbies.","b2Lift":"Connect leisure to identity, wellbeing, mastery, social connection, and time management while making persuasive recommendations.","performance":"Recommend a meaningful leisure activity for a specific person's needs and justify the recommendation against realistic constraints.","pronunciation":"Stress particles naturally in common phrasal verbs and maintain smooth rhythm in informal recommendations.","mediation":"Listen to someone's needs and constraints, then explain which hobby another classmate recommended and why.","functions":["describe a leisure habit","connect a hobby to wellbeing or identity","make a tailored recommendation"],"discourse":["needs to recommendation","benefit versus constraint","persuasive close"],"chunks":["switch off after work","make time for","sense of progress","social connection","long-term hobby"],"interactionExpressions":["Based on what you've said, ... might suit you.","The main benefit is...","The only drawback I'd mention is..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Choose the correct phrasal verb: “I ___ gardening last year because I wanted more time outdoors.”","options":["took up","gave up","put off"],"answer":"took up","tag":"grammar:phrasal-meaning"},{"q":"Choose the best option: “I ___ painting because I no longer enjoyed it.”","options":["gave up","took up","looked up"],"answer":"gave up","tag":"grammar:phrasal-meaning"},{"q":"Which sentence uses an inseparable phrasal verb correctly?","options":["She got into photography during university.","She got photography into during university.","She got into during university photography."],"answer":"She got into photography during university.","tag":"grammar:word-order"},{"q":"Which sentence correctly separates a separable phrasal verb?","options":["I tried it out before buying my own equipment.","I tried out it before buying my own equipment.","I tried before buying it out my equipment."],"answer":"I tried it out before buying my own equipment.","tag":"grammar:word-order"},{"q":"What does “keep up with” mean in “I couldn't keep up with the weekly practice”?","options":["continue at the required pace","stop permanently","begin for the first time"],"answer":"continue at the required pace","tag":"grammar:meaning"},{"q":"Complete: “My friend ___ me ___ trying a walking group, and I ended up really ___ it.”","options":["talked / into / getting into","talked / up / giving up","put / into / looking up"],"answer":"talked / into / getting into","tag":"grammar:collocation"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l20","number":20,"title":"Cultural Identity","outcome":"Explain an aspect of cultural identity to someone unfamiliar with it, respond to questions, and bridge a difference in assumptions without becoming defensive.","grammarFocus":"Wish / If Only","grammarRule":"Use Wish / If Only accurately for social-media reflection. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Social-media reflection","vocabulary":["heritage","tradition","identity","belong","preserve","diaspora"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “heritage” to its meaning.","options":["culture inherited from the past","qualities that make a person or group who they are","protect so something continues"],"answer":"culture inherited from the past","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “tradition” to its meaning.","options":["custom passed between generations","feel accepted as part of a group","people living outside their ancestral homeland"],"answer":"custom passed between generations","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “identity” to its meaning.","options":["qualities that make a person or group who they are","protect so something continues","culture inherited from the past"],"answer":"qualities that make a person or group who they are","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “belong” to its meaning.","options":["feel accepted as part of a group","people living outside their ancestral homeland","custom passed between generations"],"answer":"feel accepted as part of a group","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “preserve” to its meaning.","options":["protect so something continues","culture inherited from the past","qualities that make a person or group who they are"],"answer":"protect so something continues","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “diaspora” to its meaning.","options":["people living outside their ancestral homeland","custom passed between generations","feel accepted as part of a group"],"answer":"people living outside their ancestral homeland","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “custom passed between generations”?","options":["tradition","belong","diaspora"],"answer":"tradition","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: protect so something continues?","options":["preserve","heritage","identity"],"answer":"preserve","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “culture inherited from the past”.","answer":"heritage","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “Language is an important part of cultural ___.”","options":["identity","heritage","tradition"],"answer":"identity","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"Ifrah grew up abroad but often wishes she had learned more of her mother tongue as a child. If only her parents had spoken it more often at home, she says, she wouldn't struggle so much with it now. She wishes she knew more traditional stories, the ones her grandmother used to tell. Recently, she has started taking language classes, hoping it's not too late to reconnect with that part of her identity. When Ifrah began language classes, she expected vocabulary to be the main challenge. Instead, she found that every new expression brought questions about memory, family, and belonging. Some classmates had grown up speaking the language at home but could not read it confidently. Others, like Ifrah, understood common phrases yet hesitated to speak because they were afraid of sounding inauthentic. The class helped her see identity as more than a test that someone either passes or fails. She can value the culture she inherited while admitting that parts of it are unfamiliar. She has also become more comfortable asking older relatives to explain traditions instead of pretending she already understands them. For Ifrah, preserving culture now means active learning and conversation, not simply repeating a fixed version of the past.","audioScript":"Student: Someone told me I'm not really part of the culture because I didn't grow up speaking the language fluently. Friend: How did you respond? Student: At first I got defensive. Later I realised they were worried about language loss, but the way they said it made identity sound like a test. Friend: Could you explain that distinction to them? Student: I think so. I can say that I share the concern about preserving the language, while also explaining that people in the diaspora may connect through family, food, history, religion, or community in different combinations. Friend: That sounds more constructive than arguing about who is authentic. Student: Yes. I want the conversation to create more participation, not push people away.","questions":[{"q":"What surprised Ifrah about language classes?","options":["They raised questions about memory, family, and belonging as well as vocabulary","They contained no language learning","Everyone had exactly the same background"],"answer":"They raised questions about memory, family, and belonging as well as vocabulary","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"Why did some learners hesitate to speak?","options":["They feared sounding inauthentic","They understood nothing","They were forbidden to use the language"],"answer":"They feared sounding inauthentic","tag":"reading:reason"},{"q":"How has Ifrah's view of identity changed?","options":["She sees it as something that can include learning, gaps, and multiple forms of connection","She sees it as a simple pass-or-fail test","She believes only fluent speakers have identity"],"answer":"She sees it as something that can include learning, gaps, and multiple forms of connection","tag":"reading:development"},{"q":"What does preserving culture mean to Ifrah now?","options":["Active learning and conversation","Repeating a fixed past without questions","Avoiding older relatives"],"answer":"Active learning and conversation","tag":"reading:main-idea"},{"q":"What comment upset the student?","options":["That they were not really part of the culture because of limited language fluency","That language learning takes time","That diaspora communities are diverse"],"answer":"That they were not really part of the culture because of limited language fluency","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What concern does the student later recognise behind the comment?","options":["Worry about language loss","Concern about travel costs","A desire to end cultural learning"],"answer":"Worry about language loss","tag":"listening:inference"},{"q":"How does the student want to reframe the discussion?","options":["Preserve language while recognising different forms of cultural connection","Ignore language preservation","Decide who should be excluded"],"answer":"Preserve language while recognising different forms of cultural connection","tag":"listening:mediation"},{"q":"What outcome does the student want from the identity discussion?","options":["More participation rather than excluding people","A strict test of who is authentic","Less interest in preserving language"],"answer":"More participation rather than excluding people","tag":"listening:intercultural"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Here is something worth discussing:","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Here is something worth discussing:","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to social-media reflection","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to social-media reflection","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “people living outside their ancestral homeland”?","options":["diaspora","tradition","belong"],"answer":"diaspora","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["What do you think?","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"What do you think?","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write a 90–120 word social-media reflection about a tradition, language or part of identity you value.","minWords":110,"maxWords":130,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle wish and if only to express present wishes and past regrets.","b2Lift":"Discuss belonging, heritage, language, and changing identity while explaining cultural practices respectfully across different perspectives.","performance":"Explain an aspect of cultural identity to someone unfamiliar with it, respond to questions, and bridge a difference in assumptions without becoming defensive.","pronunciation":"Use respectful clarification intonation and avoid excessive stress that can make intercultural correction sound confrontational.","mediation":"Explain a cultural practice or identity issue to someone from a different background and clarify possible misunderstanding.","functions":["express a complex sense of belonging","explain a cultural practice","clarify an intercultural misunderstanding"],"discourse":["personal experience to broader identity","explaining without assuming shared knowledge","repairing misunderstanding"],"chunks":["cultural heritage","sense of belonging","family tradition","language loss","preserve a practice"],"interactionExpressions":["In my community, this usually means...","I can see why that might be interpreted differently.","A useful distinction is..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Choose a wish about a present situation.","options":["I wish I spoke my heritage language more fluently.","I wish I had spoken my heritage language more fluently yesterday only.","I wish I will speak my heritage language more fluently now."],"answer":"I wish I spoke my heritage language more fluently.","tag":"grammar:present-wish"},{"q":"Choose a regret about the past.","options":["If only I had asked my grandparents more questions.","If only I asked my grandparents more questions yesterday.","If only I would ask my grandparents more questions in the past."],"answer":"If only I had asked my grandparents more questions.","tag":"grammar:past-regret"},{"q":"Which sentence expresses a wish for another person's behaviour to change?","options":["I wish people would stop treating identity like a test.","I wish people stopped treating identity like a test yesterday only.","I wish people will stop treating identity like a test."],"answer":"I wish people would stop treating identity like a test.","tag":"grammar:would-wish"},{"q":"What does “I wish I knew more traditional stories” imply?","options":["I don't know as many as I would like now.","I knew all of them in the past.","I will certainly learn all of them tomorrow."],"answer":"I don't know as many as I would like now.","tag":"grammar:meaning"},{"q":"Choose the best sentence for an impossible past change but a useful present reflection.","options":["I wish I had learned more as a child, so I'm studying now.","I wish I learned more as a child, so the past will change.","I wish I would have learn more as a child."],"answer":"I wish I had learned more as a child, so I'm studying now.","tag":"grammar:reflection"},{"q":"Complete: “If only I ___ more confident speaking then; I wish I ___ so worried about making mistakes now.”","options":["had been / weren't","was / hadn't been","would be / haven't been"],"answer":"had been / weren't","tag":"grammar:contrast"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l21","number":21,"title":"Making Decisions","outcome":"Evaluate competing options in a realistic decision, ask for clarification, challenge weak assumptions, and reach a justified shared decision.","grammarFocus":"Indirect Questions / Question Tags","grammarRule":"Use Indirect Questions / Question Tags accurately for advice message/email. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Advice message/email","vocabulary":["instinct","option","commit","hesitate","indecisive","confident"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “instinct” to its meaning.","options":["a natural feeling that guides a decision","to make a firm decision or promise","unable to make decisions confidently"],"answer":"a natural feeling that guides a decision","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “option” to its meaning.","options":["one possible choice","to pause because you are uncertain","sure of your ability"],"answer":"one possible choice","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “commit” to its meaning.","options":["to make a firm decision or promise","unable to make decisions confidently","a natural feeling that guides a decision"],"answer":"to make a firm decision or promise","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “hesitate” to its meaning.","options":["to pause because you are uncertain","sure of your ability","one possible choice"],"answer":"to pause because you are uncertain","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “indecisive” to its meaning.","options":["unable to make decisions confidently","a natural feeling that guides a decision","to make a firm decision or promise"],"answer":"unable to make decisions confidently","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “confident” to its meaning.","options":["sure of your ability","one possible choice","to pause because you are uncertain"],"answer":"sure of your ability","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “one possible choice”?","options":["option","hesitate","confident"],"answer":"option","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: unable to make decisions confidently?","options":["indecisive","instinct","commit"],"answer":"indecisive","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “a natural feeling that guides a decision”.","answer":"instinct","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “Before deciding, we should ___ the pros and cons.”","options":["weigh up","instinct","option"],"answer":"weigh up","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"Nasra was offered two jobs at the same time, and she wasn't sure which to choose. She asked her mentor if she could help her weigh up the options, didn't she always give good advice? Her mentor asked her what really mattered to her — money, growth, or flexibility. In the end, Nasra realised she already knew the answer; she just needed someone to ask the right questions. Nasra's mentor did not tell her which job to choose. Instead, she asked Nasra to rank the factors that mattered: salary, learning opportunities, stability, commute, organisational culture, and long-term career direction. When Nasra did this, she discovered that she had been comparing the jobs mainly by salary because that was the easiest difference to measure. The higher-paying role offered less training and required a long commute, while the lower-paying position included mentoring and responsibilities closer to the career she wanted. The exercise did not make the decision obvious, but it made the trade-offs visible. Nasra also considered what would happen if either role changed after six months. She eventually chose the position with stronger development opportunities, not because money was unimportant but because she had clarified which benefit she was willing to sacrifice in the short term.","audioScript":"Advisor: Before we compare the two offers, what is non-negotiable for you? Nasra: I need enough income to cover my expenses, but development also matters. Advisor: Then let's not treat the highest salary as automatically best. What concerns you about Offer A? Nasra: The commute and the fact that the role seems very narrow. Advisor: And Offer B? Nasra: Lower starting pay, although the manager has offered structured mentoring. Advisor: Which risk would you regret more in a year: earning slightly less or learning very little? Nasra: Probably learning very little. Advisor: Then your decision criteria are becoming clearer. We still need to check the budget, but now we know what the real trade-off is.","questions":[{"q":"What did Nasra's mentor ask her to rank?","options":["The factors that mattered in the decision","The popularity of each company","Only the salary"],"answer":"The factors that mattered in the decision","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"Why had salary dominated Nasra's thinking?","options":["It was the easiest difference to measure","It was her only priority","Both salaries were identical"],"answer":"It was the easiest difference to measure","tag":"reading:inference"},{"q":"What trade-off did the two jobs reveal?","options":["Higher immediate pay versus stronger development opportunities","Local work versus no work","Short hours versus unemployment"],"answer":"Higher immediate pay versus stronger development opportunities","tag":"reading:main-idea"},{"q":"Why did Nasra choose the lower-paying role?","options":["She prioritised development after checking what she could sacrifice short term","She decided money never matters","The other employer withdrew the offer"],"answer":"She prioritised development after checking what she could sacrifice short term","tag":"reading:reason"},{"q":"What does the advisor ask Nasra to identify first?","options":["Her non-negotiable priorities","Which company has the better logo","What her friends prefer"],"answer":"Her non-negotiable priorities","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What concern does Nasra have about Offer A?","options":["The commute and narrow role","The mentoring programme","The low salary"],"answer":"The commute and narrow role","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What advantage does Offer B provide?","options":["Structured mentoring","The highest salary","No responsibilities"],"answer":"Structured mentoring","tag":"listening:detail"},{"q":"What does the advisor help Nasra identify?","options":["The real trade-off between income and development","That salary should never matter","That both jobs are identical"],"answer":"The real trade-off between income and development","tag":"listening:decision"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Dear Sir or Madam,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Dear Sir or Madam,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to advice message/email","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to advice message/email","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “sure of your ability”?","options":["confident","option","hesitate"],"answer":"confident","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Kind regards,","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Kind regards,","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"Write a 90–120 word advice message helping someone think through two options.","minWords":110,"maxWords":130,"humanGraded":false,"checkpoint":false},"foundation":"Recycle indirect questions and question tags for polite advice-seeking and checking.","b2Lift":"Set decision criteria, express reservations, challenge assumptions diplomatically, negotiate priorities, and move toward consensus.","performance":"Evaluate competing options in a realistic decision, ask for clarification, challenge weak assumptions, and reach a justified shared decision.","pronunciation":"Use diplomatic intonation in indirect questions, reservations, and tentative disagreement.","mediation":"Summarise each person's priorities neutrally before helping the group identify a decision everyone can accept.","functions":["clarify decision criteria","express a reservation diplomatically","negotiate priorities"],"discourse":["criteria before options","testing assumptions","moving from disagreement to consensus"],"chunks":["weigh up the options","non-negotiable priority","possible trade-off","long-term consequence","reach a decision"],"interactionExpressions":["Could you clarify what matters most to you?","My only reservation is...","If we prioritise..., then..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Choose the correct indirect question.","options":["Could you tell me what the main priority is?","Could you tell me what is the main priority?","Could you tell me what the main priority?"],"answer":"Could you tell me what the main priority is?","tag":"grammar:indirect-question"},{"q":"Which version sounds most diplomatic when asking for advice?","options":["I was wondering whether you could help me weigh up the options.","Tell me which job to take.","I wonder can you decide this for me?"],"answer":"I was wondering whether you could help me weigh up the options.","tag":"grammar:register"},{"q":"Choose the correct question tag.","options":["You've already checked the contract, haven't you?","You've already checked the contract, don't you?","You've already checked the contract, haven't they?"],"answer":"You've already checked the contract, haven't you?","tag":"grammar:question-tag"},{"q":"The speaker is genuinely uncertain. Which intonation/function is most appropriate for a question tag?","options":["Use the tag as a real check rather than assuming agreement.","Use it only as an aggressive challenge.","Treat every tag as a statement with no response expected."],"answer":"Use the tag as a real check rather than assuming agreement.","tag":"grammar:pragmatics"},{"q":"Choose the best embedded question.","options":["Do you happen to know how flexible the schedule is?","Do you happen to know how flexible is the schedule?","Do you happen to know is how flexible the schedule?"],"answer":"Do you happen to know how flexible the schedule is?","tag":"grammar:word-order"},{"q":"Complete: “I was wondering ___ you could clarify the training budget. It's included, ___?”","options":["if / isn't it","that / doesn't it","whether / hasn't it"],"answer":"if / isn't it","tag":"grammar:contrast"}]},{"id":"su-b2-l22","number":22,"title":"Looking Back, Looking Forward","outcome":"Demonstrate integrated B2 performance across reading, listening, interaction, spoken production, mediation, and independent writing.","grammarFocus":"Mixed Review","grammarRule":"Use Mixed Review accurately for independent final writing assessment. Focus on form, meaning, and one useful contrast.","finalWriting":"Independent final writing assessment","vocabulary":["fluency","progress","confidence","milestone","consistency","achievement"],"vocabularyItems":[{"type":"choice","q":"Match “fluency” to its meaning.","options":["the ability to communicate smoothly and effectively","belief in your own ability","the quality of continuing at a steady standard"],"answer":"the ability to communicate smoothly and effectively","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “progress” to its meaning.","options":["improvement over time","an important stage showing progress","something important completed successfully"],"answer":"improvement over time","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “confidence” to its meaning.","options":["belief in your own ability","the quality of continuing at a steady standard","the ability to communicate smoothly and effectively"],"answer":"belief in your own ability","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “milestone” to its meaning.","options":["an important stage showing progress","something important completed successfully","improvement over time"],"answer":"an important stage showing progress","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “consistency” to its meaning.","options":["the quality of continuing at a steady standard","the ability to communicate smoothly and effectively","belief in your own ability"],"answer":"the quality of continuing at a steady standard","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Match “achievement” to its meaning.","options":["something important completed successfully","improvement over time","an important stage showing progress"],"answer":"something important completed successfully","tag":"vocabulary:meaning"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word means “improvement over time”?","options":["progress","milestone","achievement"],"answer":"progress","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which target word best matches this idea: the quality of continuing at a steady standard?","options":["consistency","fluency","confidence"],"answer":"consistency","tag":"vocabulary:context"},{"type":"exact","q":"Type the target word meaning “the ability to communicate smoothly and effectively”.","answer":"fluency","min":1,"tag":"vocabulary:recall"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word best completes the sentence: “Daily practice requires ___.”","options":["consistency","fluency","progress"],"answer":"consistency","tag":"vocabulary:application"}],"readingText":"When Deeqa started this course, she could barely hold a two-minute conversation in English. If someone had told her she would be discussing global issues and giving business pitches in English by the end of the term, she wouldn't have believed them. She has been practising consistently, and she has made mistakes along the way — but she says that's exactly how she has learned the most. Deeqa's final challenge is different from an ordinary grammar review. She is given unfamiliar information and has to decide what matters, explain it clearly, respond to questions, and adapt when another person misunderstands her. She notices that progress is not simply the number of structures she remembers. Earlier in the course, she often knew the grammar but lost confidence when a conversation moved in an unexpected direction. Now she is better able to pause, reformulate, ask for clarification, and continue. She still makes errors, particularly when she is speaking quickly, but the errors rarely stop the message. Looking forward, Deeqa wants to become more precise when discussing unfamiliar professional topics. She plans to keep a weekly speaking routine, read longer articles, and record short summaries so that receptive input becomes active language rather than information she only recognises.","audioScript":"Assessor: You've read two short viewpoints on remote work. Can you summarise the main difference? Candidate: Yes. The first writer focuses mainly on flexibility and reduced commuting, while the second argues that fully remote teams can lose informal collaboration. Assessor: Which argument did you find stronger? Candidate: The second one was more convincing because it included examples of communication problems, although I don't think that means remote work is ineffective. A hybrid system might address both concerns. Assessor: Could you explain that recommendation to a manager who hasn't read the texts? Candidate: I would say that remote work can improve flexibility, but teams still need deliberate opportunities for fast informal discussion. So I would recommend remote work for focused tasks and regular in-person or live collaborative sessions for work that depends on rapid coordination.","questions":[{"q":"What communication problem did Deeqa have earlier in the course?","options":["She lost confidence when interaction became unexpected","She could not memorise any grammar","She refused to speak at all"],"answer":"She lost confidence when interaction became unexpected","tag":"reading:detail"},{"q":"What can she do better now when communication becomes difficult?","options":["Pause, reformulate, ask for clarification, and continue","End the conversation immediately","Repeat the same sentence louder"],"answer":"Pause, reformulate, ask for clarification, and continue","tag":"reading:development"},{"q":"What limitation does Deeqa still recognise?","options":["Precision on unfamiliar professional topics","Basic ability to introduce herself","Understanding any familiar text"],"answer":"Precision on unfamiliar professional topics","tag":"reading:self-assessment"},{"q":"Why does she plan to record short summaries of longer articles?","options":["To turn receptive input into active language","To avoid reading full texts","To memorise every sentence exactly"],"answer":"To turn receptive input into active language","tag":"reading:strategy"},{"q":"What is the main difference between the two remote-work viewpoints?","options":["One stresses flexibility while the other stresses collaboration risks","Both reject remote work completely","Both discuss salary only"],"answer":"One stresses flexibility while the other stresses collaboration risks","tag":"listening:synthesis"},{"q":"Why does the candidate find the second argument stronger?","options":["It includes examples of communication problems","It is shorter","It completely rejects flexibility"],"answer":"It includes examples of communication problems","tag":"listening:evaluation"},{"q":"What compromise does the candidate propose?","options":["A hybrid system combining focused remote work and collaborative sessions","Full-time office work only","Remote work with no live contact"],"answer":"A hybrid system combining focused remote work and collaborative sessions","tag":"listening:solution"},{"q":"What does the candidate do instead of choosing one viewpoint completely?","options":["Synthesises both concerns and proposes a hybrid response","Repeats the first source only","Refuses to make a recommendation"],"answer":"Synthesises both concerns and proposes a hybrid response","tag":"listening:mediation"}],"writing":{"builder":[{"q":"Which opening best suits this task and audience?","options":["Hello,","To whom it may concern regarding everything,","Hey!!! Send answer now."],"answer":"Hello,","tag":"writing-builder:register"},{"q":"Which content should be included?","options":["A clear purpose connected to independent final writing assessment","Unrelated private details","A long list with no purpose"],"answer":"A clear purpose connected to independent final writing assessment","tag":"writing-builder:task"},{"q":"Choose the clearest connector for adding a supporting point.","options":["In addition,","Despite because,","On the contrary of,"],"answer":"In addition,","tag":"writing-builder:cohesion"},{"q":"Which target word means “something important completed successfully”?","options":["achievement","progress","milestone"],"answer":"achievement","tag":"writing-builder:vocabulary"},{"q":"Which closing is most appropriate?","options":["Thank you.","Finish.","Reply immediately or else."],"answer":"Thank you.","tag":"writing-builder:register"}],"task":"FINAL HUMAN-GRADED TASK: Write 180–220 words reflecting on your English progress and setting a six-month goal. No automated correction before submission.","minWords":180,"maxWords":220,"humanGraded":true,"checkpoint":true},"foundation":"Recycle high-value language from the course so learners enter the final performance confidently.","b2Lift":"Integrate reception, interaction, production, mediation, discourse control, and strategic language use without relying on one grammar target.","performance":"Demonstrate integrated B2 performance across reading, listening, interaction, spoken production, mediation, and independent writing.","pronunciation":"Integrate intelligible stress, rhythm, pausing, and intonation during extended speaking rather than focusing on isolated sounds.","mediation":"Summarise and explain unfamiliar information accurately for another person as part of the final performance showcase.","functions":["synthesise course language","perform independently across skills","reflect on strengths and next targets"],"discourse":["extended organisation","source synthesis","self-monitoring and repair"],"chunks":["evidence of progress","sustained interaction","independent performance","next learning target","communication strategy"],"interactionExpressions":["The point I'd like to emphasise is...","To put that another way...","The evidence I would use is..."],"grammarItems":[{"q":"Choose the best tense combination for a course that started in the past and still affects the learner now.","options":["I've become more confident since I started the course.","I became more confident since I have started the course.","I had become more confident since I start the course."],"answer":"I've become more confident since I started the course.","tag":"grammar:mixed-review"},{"q":"Choose the best counterfactual reflection.","options":["If I hadn't practised regularly, I wouldn't be this confident now.","If I didn't practise regularly, I wouldn't have been this confident now.","If I wouldn't practise regularly, I am not confident now."],"answer":"If I hadn't practised regularly, I wouldn't be this confident now.","tag":"grammar:mixed-review"},{"q":"Which sentence reports a general view while keeping distance from it?","options":["It is often said that consistency matters more than occasional intensive study.","It often says that consistency matters more.","Consistency is said that it matter more."],"answer":"It is often said that consistency matters more than occasional intensive study.","tag":"grammar:mixed-review"},{"q":"Choose the best future plan with a clear arrangement and intention.","options":["I'm meeting my tutor next week, and I'm going to set a new speaking target.","I'll meeting my tutor next week, and I set a target.","I'm going meet my tutor next week, and I will to set a target."],"answer":"I'm meeting my tutor next week, and I'm going to set a new speaking target.","tag":"grammar:mixed-review"},{"q":"Which sentence uses an appropriate modal of deduction?","options":["Her answers are more detailed now, so she must have been practising regularly.","Her answers are more detailed now, so she can have practise regularly.","Her answers are detailed, so she must practised regularly."],"answer":"Her answers are more detailed now, so she must have been practising regularly.","tag":"grammar:mixed-review"},{"q":"Which sentence best demonstrates B2 control by linking ideas and qualifying a claim?","options":["Although I've improved considerably, I still need to become more precise when discussing unfamiliar topics.","I've improved and I'm perfect now.","I improved considerably but unfamiliar topics are no problem always."],"answer":"Although I've improved considerably, I still need to become more precise when discussing unfamiliar topics.","tag":"grammar:discourse"}]}];
